1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pope_John_XXII
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Pope John XXII''' (Latin: ''Ioannes XXII'', Italian: ''Giovanni XXII'', French: ''Jean XXII''; 1244 – 4 December 1334), born '''Jacques Duèze''' (or '''d'Euse'''), was head of the Catholic Church from 7 August 1316 to his death
2 CONC , in December 1334. He was the second and longest-reigning Avignon Pope, elected by the Conclave of Cardinals, which was assembled in Lyon. Like his predecessor, [https://www.geni.com/people/Pope-Clement-V/6000000057327773067 Clem
2 CONC ent V], Pope John centralized power and income in the Papacy and lived a princely life in Avignon.
2 CONT
2 CONT John opposed the policies of [https://www.geni.com/people/Louis-IV-Duke-of-Upper-Bavaria/4202780705000039134 Louis IV the Bavarian] as Holy Roman Emperor, which prompted Louis to invade Italy and set up an antipope, [https://www.g
2 CONC eni.com/people/Antipope-Nicholas-V/6000000186336455107 Nicholas V]. John also opposed the Franciscan understanding of the poverty of Christ and his apostles, promulgating multiple papal bulls to enforce his views. This led William
2 CONC  of Ockham to write against unlimited papal power.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following a three-year process, John canonized [https://www.geni.com/people/Saint-Thomas-Aquinas-OP/6000000004533510242 Thomas Aquinas] on 18 July 1323. One of John's sermons on the beatific vision caused controversy which lasted 
2 CONC until he retracted his views just before his death. John died in Avignon on 4 December 1334.  .. ... ....
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.britannica.com/biography/John-XXII
2 CONT https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/08431a.htm
2 CONT https://www.papalartifacts.com/portfolio-item/pope-john-xxii/
2 CONT https://www.encyclopedia.com/reference/encyclopedias-almanacs-transcripts-and-maps/john-xxii-pope
2 CONT https://www.thecatholicvoice.com/popes/pope-john-xxii
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/82762737/john_xxii
2 CONT
2 CONT Clergy. Born Jacques Arnaud Duèze (or d'Euse), he became Bishop of Fréjus in 1300 and Bishop of Avignon in 1310. Two years later he was created cardinal by Clement V , the first pope to reside in Avignon. The death of the pope in 
2 CONC 1314 was followed by a two years long interregnum. The conclave that started in 1314 in Carpentras was unable to find a pope due to disagreements between the french and italian cardinals. They had to flee when the city was besiege
2 CONC d. They dispersed to different cities. In March 1316 Louis X summoned the cardinals under different pretexts to Lyon and sent his brother Philipp as his envoy. Before the conclave could start, the king died and Philipp, now regent
2 CONC  for the unborn king, imprisoned the cardinals in the Dominican convent of Lyon, and returned to Paris. After forty days under miserable conditions, Jacques Duèze was elected pope. At 72 years old, he was expected to be a temporar
2 CONC y solution, but his pontificate would be the longest of the Avignon popes.
2 CONT
2 CONT John XXII's early disputes with the Franciscans, and Louis IV of Bavaria, whose claim to the Holy Roman Empire he disputed, merged when Louis proclaimed John XXII deposed in Rome. He chose a Franciscan Pietro Rainalducci (as Antip
2 CONC ope Nicholas V. 18, 1328. He had the papal palace in Avignon built. He canonized Louis of Toulouse in 1317 and Thomas Aquinas in 1323.
