1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [http://mesancetres.canalblog.com/archives/2011/12/08/22920256.html Arnaud-Guilhem III de Gramont]
2 CONT
2 CONT * Died in 1345.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Seigneur de Gramont et de Bidache, Souverain de Bidache, Ricombre et Maréchal de Navarre.''' (Lord de Gramont and Bidache Sovereign Bidache Ricombre and Marshal of Navarre.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Raymond-Brun III de Gramont.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and children:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Around 1320 with '''Miramonde Aspremont'''.
2 CONT
2 CONT      - Fortaner de Gramont.
2 CONT
2 CONT      - Jean de Gramont.
2 CONT
2 CONT      - Hugues (Auger Oger) de Gramont.
2 CONT
2 CONT      - Robert de Gramont.
2 CONT
2 CONT      - Brunat de Gramont.
2 CONT
2 CONT      - Arnaud de Gramont-Raimon I (-1389).
2 CONT
2 CONT Extract from the history and genealogy of the house of Gramont by Antoine-Agenor-Alfred Duke of Gramont
2 CONT
2 CONT Arnaud-Guillem de Gramont, third name, succeeded in 1312, his father, as Sovereign of Bidache Ricombre & Marshal of Navarre.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Annalles Navarre mention of Arnaud-Guilhem III, in 1312, on the occasion of the death of Louis le Hutin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip the Long, brother of Louis, who had succeeded to the throne of France, under the Salic law, to acknowledge him as King of Navarre to the detriment of his niece Jeanne. Institutions Navarroises not excluoient women from the 
2 CONC throne, and Jeanne had incontestable rights to the crown, but she could not make out that a few years later. Navarre states were far from approving this usurpation, but the resistance was scarcely possible, & the formal expression
2 CONC  of the will of the King of France, they had appointed, in 1319, a deputation of forty-two noble Navarrese to go to take the oath of fidelity, and receive one of the Kings of Navarre were accustomed to their subjects ascended the 
2 CONC throne. Philip the Long, held in France, then could not get in Navarre, and the deputation was to get him in his dominions. The details of these events is reported in the Annals of Navarre, who have even preserved the names of the
2 CONC  Lords who formed a part of the deputation. There are three prelates, six Ricombres, including Don Arnalt Guillelmo, Senor Agramont y Bidajon eight Knights, & c. [...].
2 CONT
2 CONT However, this act extorted by force, rather than spontaneous from the Navarrese, received no scripts. The nobility and the states of Navarre were afraid, and rightly so, to become a province of France, found a thousand excuses to 
2 CONC delay the departure of the deputation, and neither Philip the Long, or his brother and successor, Charles-le-Bel , could never obtain from the Navarrese, explicit gratitude of their sovereignty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles le Bel had died in 1327, Philippe de Valois, King of France, made vain attempts to maintain the same way queses predecessors, the crown of Navarre. The Shadows-Ricos, the aldermen & the clergy met in Puente de la Reyna, an
2 CONC d solemnly pronounced the forfeiture of the King of France, reconnoissant to their rightful Queen Joan, only daughter of Louis le Hutin, wife of Philip the -Good, Count of Evreux. A deputation was instructed to bring the Countess 
2 CONC of Evreux decree of proclamation, and the couple were quick to respond to the wishes of Navarrese. Philippe de Valois, in the presence of this national demonstration, realized that he had better to do was to give up the throne of 
2 CONC Navarre in favor of his cousin, and Navarre continued, under the scepter of Jeanne, the course of his independent existence.
2 CONT
2 CONT The return of Jeanne, the joy of Navarrois was extreme, and he was immediately carried to the solemnity of the coronation of the new Queen, according to the forms prescribed by the foral the Kingdom (1328). Arnaud-Guilhem attended
2 CONC  the coronation, which took place in Pamplona, ​​and according to the Annals of Navarre, he was one of those who, as Ricos-Shadows, received their hands oath to the Queen & King.
2 CONT
2 CONT "The following year, September 22, 1329, Arnaud William III, Lord de Gramont, renewed in Pamplona, ​​to the King Don Philip III & Noble Queen Jeanne, his wife, the homage of his predecessors Gramont , all in the presence of noble:
2 CONC  Aymero, Lord Arthiat, sent the Kings to the States, and Don John Martiniz Medrano & others .... "
2 CONT
2 CONT At that time, the city of Bayonne who practiced some atrocities against the inhabitants of the territory of Bidache, Arnaud-Guilhem armed himself against her and there was war between them for nearly six months. The quarrel ended 
2 CONC with an arbitral award in the year 1328, made by Viscount of Orte, parent, marriage, the Lord de Gramont, and which was common arbiter of both parties.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, a few years later, the harassment began all over again, and the fight took much larger proportions. The city of Bayonne is in league with several Lord of Navarre, attempted to prohibit the Lordship of Bidache access to th
2 CONC e port, preventing navigation Bidouze. Among the allies found themselves Bayonne Lords of Albret, already powerful in Navarre, and the Lords of Luxury, whose family with that of Donnesain, composoit almost all Basque nobility. [..
2 CONC .]
2 CONT
2 CONT The old animosity of Lords Guiche against their neighbors Bidache , ought naturally rank among the allies of Bayonne, and the specific benefits they retiroient the geographical position of the chateau de Guiche, overlooking the co
2 CONC nfluence of the Adour & the Bidouze in faisoient to the Lord de Gramont, formidable opponents.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, Arnaud-Guilhem up, its areas, a large enough army to resist the league that was formed against him. The King & Queen of Navarre long remained strangers to these feudal struggles, as in 1335, the Lord of Gramont jouissoit 
2 CONC yet their trust. [...]
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1342, Arnaud-Guilhem who fleeced some land under the Crown, the King of Navarre was grab his Château de Gramont, and products tolls Roncesvalles.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gaston IX, Count of Foix & Viscount of Bearn, which Arnaud-Guilhem had been able to support the household, is entremit to the King in his favor, and the Lord de Gramont, restored to favor, was not slow to recover what it had been 
2 CONC removed.
2 CONT
2 CONT The same year, he renewed the hands of the King of Navarre, the tributes from its ancestors for the Château de Gramont, and its areas of Haunte Navarre. The deed is dated in Pamplona July 10, 1342, and signed, among other Lords, b
2 CONC y Auger or Oger de Gramont, brother said Arnaud-Guilhem. In the Spanish version, it gives Auger qualification Doncel , that is to say Damoiseau or aspiring Knight. [...]
2 CONT
2 CONT "In 1342 years, for some excesses committed or charged to Arnaud William, Lord de Gramont & Bidache & his companions, on land & in the court of the King of Navarre, King Don Philip ordered that they should put receiver on the cast
2 CONC les of Gramont & Bidache & product toll of Roncesvalles, which had been given him by the Kings, his predecessors. Despite his displeasure, the King could not help but burst goodness. For the prayer of the Count of Foix and of some
2 CONC  other relatives of the King, he calmed down & ordered the lifting of the receivership, and Arnaud William & swore renewed old tributes which the Archbishop of Sens, Lieutenant & first cousin King, took text to make it a little re
2 CONC primand. Among other things, the Lord swore he changes it Gramont nothing to arms of the King, who were the Château de Gramont, he arboreroit other banners, all Whenever the Governor Rio or ordain him, and he's always tiendroit Ch
2 CONC ataeu to them. The act was done on the 10th of July in the year 1342, in the Chapel of St. Gregory, in the presence of Archbishop, Lieutenant King, Sir Robert Maylllart Lieutenant Governor, Don John of Periz Arbeyza, Don Michael O
2 CONC rtiz Miranda, Knights, Don Michel Sanguès Alcalde of the Court, Auger de Gramont, Doncel brother said Lord de Gramont & other . "
2 CONT
2 CONT It would be sufficient for this act of homage, if it was followed by several others, to prove that the Lords of Montaut, appearing with the name of Gramont, from the Lords of Lomagne not possédoient, and n ' have never possessed t
2 CONC he fiefs of the House in Navarre.
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite the release, as it immediately, its areas of Navarre, a moment sequestered, invasion, led by the King of Navarre to the properties of one of its oldest & faithful lords, hired Arnaud-Guilhem to offer its services King of F
2 CONC rance, and from that moment he followed Philip of Valois in his wars against Edward of England, both in Guyenne in Normandy, and Philip VI held him to his court in its armed & confidence & large honor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Arnaud-Guilhem III had married Miramonde Aspremont from Orte House, which is one of the oldest & most famous of Gascony. Aspremont door gules a silver cross. [...]
2 CONT
2 CONT Arnaud-Guilhem III died in the year 1345.
