1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roger-Bernard_de_Périgord
2 CONT Roger-Bernard de Périgord (c. 1299 – 1361) was a French nobleman, Count of Périgord from 1336 to 1361.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT The youngest son of Hélie IX de Périgord and Brunissende de Foix, he was given the first name of his maternal grandfather, Roger-Bernard III de Foix. In 1334 he succeeded his elder brother Archambaud IV, who died without issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT His other brother, Hélie, already a bishop, had been appointed cardinal in 1331 by Pope John XXII. His sister Agnes became Duchess of Durazzo by marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Between 1334 and 1340; in competition with Mathe d'Albret, he tried to keep the lordship of Bergerac of which she was the heiress as the wife of her elder brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT With the outbreak of hostilities in the Hundred Years' War, the Périgord was regularly attacked, notably by Henry, Earl of Derby in 1345.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1356, Roger-Bernard was confronted with the ride of the Black Prince, who finally entered Périgueux without encountering any resistance. This cavalry ended with the English victory at Poitiers and the capture of the French king
2 CONC  John II.
2 CONT
2 CONT As a result of the Treaty of Brétigny (1360), the Périgord came under the sovereignty of the King of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage and descendants
2 CONT He married on 3 February 1339 Éléonore de Vendôme († c. 1350), daughter of Bouchard VI, Count of Vendôme and Alix of Brittany, and had:
2 CONT
2 CONT Archambaud V of Périgord († 1398), Count of Périgord after his father.
2 CONT Talleyrand de Périgord († 1371), lord of Bergerac.
2 CONT Jeanne de Périgord († 1366), wife of Jean II d'Armagnac ;
2 CONT Hélène de Périgord, without marriage.
2 CONT Éléonore de Périgord, wife of Gaillard de Durfort, lord of Duras.
2 CONT Marguerite de Périgord, wife of Renaud VI de Pons.
2 CONT
