1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Roger IV of Foix
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Roger IV (died 24 February 1265), son and successor of Roger-Bernard II the Great, was the seventh count of Foix from 1241 to his death. His reign began with the south again at war with the north in France and, though he was reluc
2 CONC tant to join his father's old ally, Raymond VII of Toulouse, in revolt and he did not aid the king of England, Henry III, in his Saintonge War.
2 CONT
2 CONT Roger IV was a vassal of both the count of Toulouse (for his county of Foix) and the king of France (for his Carcassonne lands), then Louis IX. His loyalty to the king, however, took precedence and war broke out with Raymond VII (
2 CONC October 1242). Though Raymond was forced to submit to the king on November 30, the war with Roger continued until January 1243, when the king ended it. The count of Foix was now solely a vassal of the king and Raymond spent the re
2 CONC st of his life (until 1249) trying to retrieve Roger's homage, to no avail.
2 CONT
2 CONT Like his father, Roger IV had troubles with the church and the bishop of Urgel in particular and, in 1257, he successfully released the viscounty of Castelbon from the bishop's jurisdiction. In February 1245, he gave many freedoms
2 CONC  to his subjects and he signed paréages with the abbots of Mas-d'Azil (1246), Boulbonne (1253), and Combelongue (1255). In 1251, he built the church at Boulbonne, transferred his ancestors' remains there, and defended it against t
2 CONC he exactions of numerous enemies. He persecuted Catharism, the heresy of his mother, Ermesinda of Castlebon, in order not to be at odds with the Inquisition. He died on February 24, 1265, and was interred in Boulbonne next to his 
2 CONC father.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Marriage and children
2 CONT
2 CONT Roger married Brunissenda of Cardona, a daughter of Ramon VIII, Viscount of Cardona. They had six children:
2 CONT
