1 OCCU First Viscount of Perellós and second of Rueda, Butler, chamberlain and adviser to Infante Joan,  senyor de Voló, Tuïr and Montesquiu (1381), lord of Ceret, lord of Estagell, Rodés and Llo, captain general of Roussillon, senyor de
2 CONC  Rueda, Perellós and
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From Wikipedia 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ramon de Perellós (probably born in Perellós Castle and died after 1424) was a Catalan nobleman and writer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Plaque commemorating the pilgrimage of Ramon de Perellós to purgatory, on the wall of the church of Sant Miquel de Perellós He was the first viscount of Perellós and the second viscount of Roda . He was the son of Francesc de Pere
2 CONC llós , chamberlain and admiral of King Charles V of France , and brother of Miquel de Perellós , he was educated at the French court until 1380, when he passed into the service of the infant Joan . Despite being Viscount of Perell
2 CONC ós, it is not stated in any document that he resided in the castle of which he was the owner.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1374 King Peter III the Ceremonious rescued him from the king of Granada, who had imprisoned him on his return from England . Between 1377 and 1378 the king of Cyprus served as a galley skipper . In 1384 he was appointed govern
2 CONC or and captain general of Roussillon , a life charge. He carried out different diplomatic missions with Charles VI of France , with Pope Benedict XIII , in Cyprus ...
2 CONT
2 CONT When John I died, he was in Avignon to investigate whether Catalonia would be invaded by the Count of Armanyac. As he was part of the king's council, he was included, like everyone else, in the process initiated by the new queen M
2 CONC aria de Luna, who had to clarify how the king had died and whether there had been any embezzlement. Like everyone else, he was acquitted of all charges.
2 CONT
2 CONT Between 8 September 1397 and March 1398 he made a trip to Ireland to visit St. Patrick's Purgatory . This trip was key to writing his work Journey to Purgatory .
2 CONT
2 CONT He continued in the service of the new king Martí l'Humà , where he again carried out various diplomatic missions. During the interregnum he clearly defined himself as an Urgellist, and militarily defended the aspirations of James
2 CONC  II of Urgell . In 1420 he took part in the defense of Naples with Orso Orsini against the Angevin troops of Muzio Attendolo Sforza . [1] In 1424 he commanded with Frederick of Luna a flock that collected Peter of Aragon , the kin
2 CONC g's brother, who resisted in Naples after the failure of the naval campaign of Alfonso the Magnanimous . [2] The fleet followed the coast untilGenoa , blocking it, and intervening in the ports of Lestri , Bonifacio and Portofiu .
2 CONT
2 CONT The Journey to Purgatory is a work in which the author seeks to defend himself from accusations of having let King John I die without spiritual help and had therefore been convicted. What he does is visit the purgatory of St. Patr
2 CONC ick, which according to legend was in Ireland, and see how the deceased king is there, waiting to go to heaven. It is a work where the author stands as a witness to certify what he sees and believes; above all he mentions many wit
2 CONC nesses who certify his act. It has some coincidences, especially plot and ideological background, with The Dream of Bernat Metge .
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Esclarmonda or Elvira de Rocabertí, daughter of Viscount Felip Dalmau I of Rocabertí , with whom he had at least two children, who also accompanied him on his trip to Ireland.
2 CONT
2 CONT ____________________
2 CONT
2 CONT From the Great Catalan encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Ramon de Perellós
2 CONT
2 CONT Diplomat and writer. ?, ? - ?, After 1419 First Viscount of Perellós and second of Rueda. Son of Francesc de Perellós and probably Caterina. He was educated at the French court, where he was page of Charles V, in whose service his
2 CONC  father was. Enric de Trastàmara donated Igualada to him, but he renounced it when he inherited the viscounty of Roda or Rueda (1370). He was part of the noble league against the viscounts of Cardona and Castellbò. He was sent to 
2 CONC England to negotiate an alliance with the Duke of Lancaster. Returning from Sant Jaume de Galicia, he was taken prisoner in the kingdom of Granada and rescued by Peter III of Catalonia-Aragon (1374). The king of Cyprus (1377-78) s
2 CONC erved as patron of a galley. Butler, chamberlain and adviser to Infante Joan, he sold him the places of Voló, Tuïr and Montesquiu (1381) and sent him to the courts of Avignon, of France and the Dukes of Bar over questions of the d
2 CONC owry of the future Queen Violant (1382-87). He was lord of Ceret and the king granted him jurisdiction to prosecute criminals in his places of Millars, Sant Marçal, Perellós, etc. (1387); he bought the castle of Estagell from Bern
2 CONC at de Senesterra (1390). He was made captain general of Roussillon, for life (1389), and captain of three galleys that were armed for Pope Benedict XIII (1390). John I made him viscount of Perellós (1391) and sold him the jurisdic
2 CONC tion of Rueda, Perellós and Montner. He was one of the tutors of his cousin Elionor de Perellós (1390). He acquired the castle of Rodés and the place of Llo (1393). In 1396 he was sent to deal with the Count of Armanyac and the Ki
2 CONC ng of France in order to prevent the invasion.
2 CONT
2 CONT The process in the Council of John I and the Journey to Purgatory When John I died, in May of this year he was in Avignon and was included in the process of the Council of John I by which many officials and courtiers were accused 
2 CONC of various acts that had motivated the death of the monarch, which happened without confession, implicit and repeated accusation of the alleged condemnation of his soul. In order to free himself from this accusation, he devised an
2 CONC  original and medieval discourse: he proposed a trip to Purgatory, where, if he found the soul of John, it would be shown that he was on the way to salvation. He presented the project to Pope Benedict XIII, who tried to dissuade h
2 CONC im; in 1397 in Paris King Charles VI recommended him to his son-in-law Richard II of England; this one, in Oxford, extended a safe-conduct to him to transfer it to Ireland and to visit the legendary onePurgatory of St. Patrick , w
2 CONC hich was to do with a retinue of twenty men. In Dublin he was provided with guides who took him to Ulster where he was very well received by King Nelan O'Neill. On the island of Lake Derg (County Donegal) he reached the entrance t
2 CONC o the cave of Purgatory: he made a will, attended a Mass for his own soul and knighted his two sons, the Englishman Thomas Agut and the Valencian Pere Maça. He entered the cave with the Norman knight Guillaume de Courry. On his re
2 CONC turn he spent Christmas with King Nelan, was received again by Richard II, attended a feast at the court of Paris, and returned to Avignon. This curious adventure, rigorously historical, was narrated in his book Journey to Purgato
2 CONC ry, a translation of Saltrey's work, except for the beginning and the end, where he relates the round trip of the expedition and the customs of the Irish, and an addition of about fifteen lines, where he explains that between the 
2 CONC the purging souls of the fourth camp met King John I, with whom he conversed and told him that he was "on the way to salvation", the Franciscan from Girona Francesc Despuig and his niece Aldonça de Queralt. The fundamental intenti
2 CONC on of the book is, obviously, in this passage where the author exhibits the proof that the soul of John I has not been damned, a curious attitude parallel to Lo somniby Bernat Metge, also included in the process of 1396 and which,
2 CONC  although in a modern Renaissance attitude, pretends that the specter of the king appears to him, which makes him agree that he is in purgatory, on the way to salvation.
2 CONT
2 CONT The last few years Acquitted of the process ( process of the Council of John I), Ramon de Perellós went to Sicily with the army of King Martí. When Benedict XIII encountered difficulties in Avignon, as his lieutenant, he went to P
2 CONC aris and obtained for the pope the protection of the troops of the Duke of Orleans (1398-1401). He spent a few years in the pontifical service. On his return, he was one of the representatives of the Catalan parliament in Aragon (
2 CONC 1410) and was one of the procurators of the count of Urgell, first in the courts of Barcelona and then in Lleida, where he swore allegiance to the count to Ferdinand I (1412). . The same count sent him to the head of the Gascon co
2 CONC mpanies that were to help the governor of Valencia, but they arrived late at the battle of Morvedre, although in Castellón he was able to disperse the Castilian troops. He intervened in favor of the count at the court of Tortosa (
2 CONC 1413). He was lord of Algerri, a place that the count had sold him. Member of the Generalitat de Catalunya in the triennium of 1416-19, he must have died shortly afterwards. Widower of the wife Sibylla, he married Violant de Luna,
2 CONC  who survived him.
