1 BIRT
2 DATE 1401
2 PLAC France
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1465
1 OCCU Lieutenant Général de Charles VII en Quercy
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} In 1395, on the death of Jean Ier de Castelnau-Calmont, the property passed to his nephew, Pons Ier de Castelnau-Caylus († 1419) son of Hélène de Castelnau7, grandson of Hugues III and husband of Catherine de Roque
2 CONC feuil (January 14, 1396). He pays homage in 1396 to King Charles VI.
2 CONT He was succeeded by Antoine de Castelnau-Caylus (1419-1465), appointed in 1442 lieutenant general of King Charles VII in Quercy.
2 CONT Jean de Castelnau-Calmont was bishop of Cahors between 1438 and 1460. He brought together the States of Quercy several times at the Château de Castelnau. He took the lead of the troops chasing the English from Quercy.
2 CONT Then comes Jean II (1465-1505), son of Antoine, who was chamberlain to Louis XI and supported him in his war against Jean V of Armagnac. He wanted the construction of the church of Castelnau. Built by his son Jacques, it became a 
2 CONC collegiate following a bull from Pope Julius II.
2 CONT In 1499, Jacques de Castelnau-Caylus married Françoise de La Tour d'Auvergne, daughter of the viscount of Turenne. The bride brings as a dowry the seigneuries of Gagnac and Bétaille. In 1505, he paid homage to King Louis XII for a
2 CONC ll of his fiefs. He accompanied him in 1512-1513 during the Italian wars. He returned to Castelnau in 1514, shortly before his death. Jean III de Castelnau-Caylus, second son of Jean II, succeeds his brother, but he dies childless
2 CONC .
2 CONT The third son of Pons Ier de Castelnau-Caylus married Antoinette Guilhem de Clermont-Lodève. He collects the goods of the house of Clermont-Lodève from which he takes the name."
2 CONT [https://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/Château_de_Castelnau-Bretenoux]
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.thepeerage.com/p20825.htm#i208249
