2 CONT Mathilde of Angoulême From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Mathilde of Angoulême (also Mahaut; after marriage Taillifer) (1181–1233) was the sole daughter of Wulgrin III, Count of Angoulême.[1] After the death of her father, the title passed to her uncle, William VII of Angoulême. After h
2 CONC er marriage to Hugh IX of Lusignan,[1] she became his consort, and the centre of the dynastic struggle between the Kings of England and France, after the death of Aymer of Angoulême. The defeat of John of England, ensured that the
2 CONC  title passed to the House of Lusignan, eventually falling to the House of Valois and to the crown of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT  Vincent 1999, p. 171.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources Vincent, Nicholas (1999). "Isabella of Angouleme:John's Jezebel". In Church, S.D. (ed.). King John: New Interpretations. Boydell Press.
2 CONT --------------------
