1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Isabella of Angoulême (French : Isabelle d'Angoulême; 1188 - 31 May 1246) was Countess of Angoulême  and queen consort of England .  Queen of England She was the only daughter and heir of Aymer Taillefer , Count of
2 CONC  Angoulême , by Alice de Courtenay . Her paternal grandparents were William IV of Angoulême , Count of Angouleme and Marguerite de Turenne. Her maternal grandparents were Pierre de Courtenay  and Elizabeth de Courtenay. Her matern
2 CONC al great-grandfather was King Louis VI of France . She became Countess of Angoulême  in her own right in 1202, by which time she was already queen of England. Her marriage to King John took place on 24 August 1200, at Bordeaux , a
2 CONC  year after he annulled  his first marriage to Isabel of Gloucester . Isabella was originally betrothed to Hugh le Brun, Count of Lusignan, son of the then Count of La Marche. As a result of John's temerity in taking her as his se
2 CONC cond wife, King Philip II of France confiscated all of their French lands, and armed conflict ensued. At the time of her marriage to John, the 12-year-old Isabella was already renowned for her beauty and has sometimes been called 
2 CONC the Helen  of the Middle Ages  by historians. However, her marriage to John cannot be said to have been successful, in part because she was much younger than her husband and had a fiery character to match his. Second marriage When
2 CONC  John died in 1216, Isabella was still in her twenties. She returned to France  and in 1220, proceeded to marry Hugh X of Lusignan  Count of La Marche . It is unclear whether it had been Hugh X or his father to whom Isabella had b
2 CONC een betrothed before her marriage to King John. By Hugh X, Isabella had nine more children. Their eldest son Hugh XI of Lusignan  succeeded his father as Count of La Marche  and Count of Angouleme  in 1249. Death and burial Isabel
2 CONC la was accused of plotting against King Louis IX of France  in 1244; she fled to Fontevrault Abbey , where she died on 31 May 1246, and was buried there. At her own insistence, she was first buried in the churchyard, as an act of 
2 CONC repentance for her many misdeeds. On a visit to Fontevrault, her son King Henry III of England  was shocked to find her buried outside the Abbey and ordered her immediately moved inside. She was finally placed beside Henry II  and
2 CONC  Eleanor of Aquitaine . Afterwards, most of her many children, having few prospects in France, set sail for England and the court of Henry, their half-brother.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Isabella of Angoulême From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella of Angoulême (Fr. Isabelle d'Angoulême ; c. 1187 – May 31, 1246) was countess of Angoulême and queen consort of England. She was the only daughter and heir of Aymer Taillifer, Count of Angoulême, by Alix de Courtenay; her
2 CONC  maternal great-grandfather was King Louis VI of France. She became Countess of Angoulême in her own right in 1202, by which time she was already queen of England. Her marriage to King John took place on August 24, 1200, at Bordea
2 CONC ux, a year after he annulled his first marriage. At the time of this marriage Isabella was aged about thirteen, and her beauty was renowned; she is sometimes called the "Helen" of the Middle Ages by historians. It could not be sai
2 CONC d to have been a successful marriage, as Isabella was much younger than her husband and had a fiery character to match his. Before their marriage, she had been betrothed to Hugh X of Lusignan[1], son of the then Count of La Marche
2 CONC . As a result of John's temerity in taking her as his second wife, King Philip II of France confiscated all his French lands, and armed conflict ensued. When John died in 1216, Isabella was still in her twenties. She returned to F
2 CONC rance and in 1220 proceeded to marry Hugh X of Lusignan, now Count of La Marche, her former fiancé. Isabella was accused of plotting against the French king in 1244; she fled to Fontevrault Abbey, where she died on May 31, 1246, a
2 CONC nd was buried there. Afterwards most of her many children, having few prospects in France, set sail for England and the court of their half-brother King Henry III.
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT With King John of England: 5 children, all of whom survived into adulthood, including: King Henry III of England (b. 1207 – d. 1272) Richard, Earl of Cornwall and King of the Romans (b. 1209 – d. 1272) Joan (b. 1210 – d. 1238), th
2 CONC e wife of King Alexander II of Scotland Isabella (b. 1214 – d. 1241), the wife of Emperor Frederick II Eleanor (b. 1215 – d. 1275), who would marry William Marshal, 2nd Earl of Pembroke
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Isabelle went into hiding (inside the abbey) due to her being blamed for the death of her husband, John. She remained in hideing (in a secret chamber) until her death. She was buried, upon her request, in the open cemetery (common
2 CONC  graveyard) at Fontevrault. Some years later her son, Henry III, moved her body into the choir of the Abbey Church & commissioned the fine effigy which is the only near contemporary likeness of her.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Isabella of Angoulême (French: Isabelle d'Angoulême) was Countess of Angoulême and queen consort of England. She became Countess of Angoulême in her own right in 1202, by which time she was already queen of England. Her marriage t
2 CONC o King John took place on 24 August 1200, at Bordeaux, a year after he annulled his first marriage to Isabel of Gloucester. At the time of this marriage Isabella was aged about twelve, and her beauty was renowned; she is sometimes
2 CONC  called the "Helen" of the Middle Ages by historians.
2 CONT
2 CONT It could not be said to have been a successful marriage, as Isabella was much younger than her husband and had a fiery character to match his. Before their marriage, she had been betrothed to Hugh le Brun, Count of Lusignan, son o
2 CONC f the then Count of La Marche. As a result of John's temerity in taking her as his second wife, King Philip II of France confiscated all his French lands, and armed conflict ensued. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She had five children with King John, including our ancestor King Henry III.
2 CONT
2 CONT When John died in 1216, Isabella was still in her twenties. She returned to France and in 1220 proceeded to marry Hugh X of Lusignan, now Count of La Marche, her former fiancé. By him, Isabella had nine more children, including ou
2 CONC r ancestor Alice of Lusignan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella was accused of plotting against King Louis IX of France in 1244; she fled to Fontevrault Abbey, where she died on 31 May 1246, and was buried there. At her own insistence she was first buried in the churchyard, as an act 
2 CONC of repentance for her many misdeeds. On a visit to Fontevrault her son King Henry III of England was shocked to find her buried outside the Abbey and ordered her immediately moved inside.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella was our ancestor through two distinct descent lines--through her son King Henry III and through her daughter Alice of Lusignan.
2 CONT
2 CONT See http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isabella_of_Angoul%C3%AAme for more information.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT She was the only daughter and heir of Aymer Taillefer, Count of Angoulême, by Alix de Courtenay. Her paternal grandparents were William V Taillefer, Count of Angouleme and Marguerite de Turenne. Her maternal grandparents were Pier
2 CONC re de Courtenay and Elizabeth de Courtenay. Her maternal great-grandfather was King Louis VI of France. She became Countess of Angoulême in her own right in 1202, by which time she was already queen of England. Her marriage to Kin
2 CONC g John took place on August 24, 1200, at Bordeaux, a year after he annulled his first marriage to Isabel of Gloucester. At the time of this marriage Isabella was aged about twelve, and her beauty was renowned; she is sometimes cal
2 CONC led the "Helen" of the Middle Ages by historians.
2 CONT
2 CONT It could not be said to have been a successful marriage, as Isabella was much younger than her husband and had a fiery character to match his. Before their marriage, she had been betrothed to Hugh X of Lusignan[2], son of the then
2 CONC  Count of La Marche. As a result of John's temerity in taking her as his second wife, King Philip II of France confiscated all his French lands, and armed conflict ensued.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT 24.08.1200 - 19.10.1216 Queen of England 16.06.1202 - 31.05.1246 Countess of Angouleme
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella of Angoulême (French: Isabelle d'Angoulême, IPA: [izabɛl dɑ̃ɡulɛm]; 1188 – 31 May 1246) was suo jure Countess of Angoulême and queen consort of England as the second wife of King John. She was queen from 24 August 1200 un
2 CONC til John's death on 19 October 1216. She had five children by the king including his heir Henry who succeeded John as Henry III of England. In 1220, Isabella married secondly Hugh X of Lusignan, Count of La Marche, by whom she had
2 CONC  another nine children. In 1241 Isabella formed a conspiracy against King Louis IX of France, after being publicly snubbed by his mother, Blanche of Castile for whom she had a deep-seated hatred.[1] In 1244, after the plot had fai
2 CONC led, Isabella was accused of attempting to poison the king, and to avoid arrest, sought refuge in Fontevraud Abbey where she died two years later at the age of about 58.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Married at age 12. Renowned for her beauty After John died, returned to France Married Hugh X of Lusigan, Count of La Marche in 1220 Five children by John and nine by Hugh
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Isabella of Angoulenne Taillefer Widow of John Lackland King of England (1167-?) (King James) and 2nd husband ? Hugh le Lusignan was her 3rd husband.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isabella_of_Angoul%C3%AAme
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Isabella of Angoulême (Fr. Isabelle d'Angoulême ; c. 1187 – May 31, 1246) was countess of Angoulême and queen consort of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the only daughter and heir of Aymer Taillifer, Count of Angoulême, by Alix de Courtenay; her maternal great-grandfather was King Louis VI of France. She became Countess of Angoulême in her own right in 1202, by which time 
2 CONC she was already queen of England. Her marriage to King John took place on August 24, 1200, at Bordeaux, a year after he annulled his first marriage. At the time of this marriage Isabella was aged about thirteen, and her beauty was
2 CONC  renowned; she is sometimes called the "Helen" of the Middle Ages by historians.
2 CONT
2 CONT It could not be said to have been a successful marriage, as Isabella was much younger than her husband and had a fiery character to match his. Before their marriage, she had been betrothed to Hugh X of Lusignan[1], son of the then
2 CONC  Count of La Marche. As a result of John's temerity in taking her as his second wife, King Philip II of France confiscated all his French lands, and armed conflict ensued.
2 CONT
2 CONT When John died in 1216, Isabella was still in her twenties. She returned to France and in 1220 proceeded to marry Hugh X of Lusignan, now Count of La Marche, her former fiancé.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella was accused of plotting against the French king in 1244; she fled to Fontevrault Abbey, where she died on May 31, 1246, and was buried there. At her own insistance she was first buried in the churchyard, as an act of repe
2 CONC ntance for her many misdeeds. On a visit to Fontevrault her son Henry III was shocked to find her buried outside the Abbey and ordered her immediately moved inside. She was finally placed beside Henry II and Eleanor of Aquitaine. 
2 CONC Afterwards most of her many children, having few prospects in France, set sail for England and the court of their half-brother King Henry III.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Isabella of Angouleme married John Lackland of England, son of Eleanor of Aquitaine and Henry II of England. John had put aside his first wife in 1198. Isabella was twelve years old at her marriage to John.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1202, Isabella's father died, and she became Countess of Angouleme in her own right.
2 CONT
2 CONT The marriage of Isabella and John was not an easy one. John was infatuated with his young and beautiful wife, but they both were reported to have engaged in adultery, and to have had strong tempers which they used on each other. W
2 CONC hen John suspected Isabella of having had an affair, he had her suspected lover hanged and then dangled above her bed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella and John had five children before John died in 1216. At John's death, Isabella's quick action had her son Henry crowned at John's death, in Gloucester where they were at the time.
2 CONT
2 CONT John and Isabella had 5 children together:
2 CONT
2 CONT # King Henry III (b. October 1, 1207)
2 CONT # Richard, Earl of Cornwall, King of the Romans
2 CONT # Joan (married Alexander II of Scotland)
2 CONT # Isabella (married Emperor Frederick II)
2 CONT # Eleanor (married William Marshall and then Simon de Montfort)
2 CONT
2 CONT ='''Epilogue'''=
2 CONT
2 CONT After John's death Isabella went on to marry Hugh X of Lusignan, Count of La Marche.  They had many scandalous adventures together, some of which you may read for yourself if you are so inclined.
2 CONT
2 CONT *By her own prior arrangement, Isabella was first buried in the Fontevraud Abbey's churchyard, as an act of repentance for her many misdeeds.  On a visit to Fontevraud, her son King Henry III of England was shocked to find her bur
2 CONC ied outside the Abbey and ordered her immediately moved inside. She was finally placed beside Henry II and Eleanor of Aquitaine.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Isabella of Angoulême (French: Isabelle d'Angoulême, IPA: [izabɛl dɑ̃ɡulɛm]; 1188[1] – 31 May 1246) was suo jure Countess of Angoulême and queen consort of England as the second wife of King John. She was queen from 24 August 1200
2 CONC  until John's death on 19 October 1216. She had five children by the king including his heir Henry  who succeeded John as Henry III of England. In 1220, Isabella married secondly the man to whom she had been originally betrothed, 
2 CONC Hugh X of Lusignan, Count of La Marche, by whom she had another nine children. Hugh had been promised to her eldest daughter, Joan, but the latter was instead married to King Alexander II of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1241, Isabella formed a conspiracy against King Louis IX of France, after being publicly snubbed by his mother, Blanche of Castile for whom she had a deep-seated hatred.[2] In 1244, after the plot had failed, Isabella was accus
2 CONC ed of attempting to poison the king, and to avoid arrest, sought refuge in Fontevraud Abbey where she died two years later at the age of about 58.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isabella_of_Angoul%C3%AAme
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabella became Countess of Angoulême in her own right on 16 June 1202, by which time she was already queen of England. Her marriage to King John took place on 24 August 1200, at Bordeaux, a year after he annulled his first marria
2 CONC ge to Isabel of Gloucester. She was crowned queen in an elaborate ceremony on 9 October at Westminster Abbey in London. Isabella was originally betrothed to Hugh IX le Brun, Count of Lusignan,[2] son of the then Count of La Marche
2 CONC . As a result of John's temerity in taking her as his second wife, King Philip II of France confiscated all of their French lands, and armed conflict ensued.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time of her marriage to John, the 12-year-old Isabella was already renowned for her beauty[3] and has sometimes been called the Helen of the Middle Ages by historians.[4] However, her marriage to John cannot be said to have
2 CONC  been successful, in part because she was much younger than her husband and possessed a volatile temper to match his own. King John, however, was deeply infatuated with his young, beautiful wife; he neglected his state affairs to 
2 CONC spend time with Isabella, often remaining in bed with her until noon, although it was the custom for kings to rise at five o'clock in the morning to commence their duties. The common people began to term her a "siren" or "Messalin
2 CONC a", although they were pleased with her beauty.[5] Her mother-in-law, Eleanor of Aquitaine readily accepted her as John's wife.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT On 1 October 1207 at Winchester Castle, Isabella gave birth to a son and heir who was named Henry after the King's father, Henry II. He was quickly followed by another son, Richard, 1st Earl of Cornwall, King of the Romans; and th
2 CONC ree daughters, Joan, Isabel, and Eleanor. All five children survived into adulthood, and would make illustrious marriages; all but Joan would produce offspring of their own.
