1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Louis de Bourbon'''1 
2 CONT M, #113677, '''b. 1388, d. 1404'''
2 CONT Last Edited=10 May 2003
2 CONT '''     Louis de Bourbon was born in 1388.1 He was the son of''' Louis II de Bourbon, Duc de Bourbon and Anne de Clermont, Comtesse de Forez.1 '''He died in 1404.'''1
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT 1.[S16] Jirí Louda and Michael MacLagan, Lines of Succession: Heraldry of the Royal Families of Europe, 2nd edition (London, U.K.: Little, Brown and Company, 1999), table 68. Hereinafter cited as Lines of Succession.
2 CONT https://thepeerage.com/p11368.htm#i113677
2 CONT _______________
2 CONT '''Louis de Bourbon'''
2 CONT '''Birth	1378'''
2 CONT '''Death	12 Sep 1404 (aged 25–26)'''
2 CONT '''Burial	Couvent des Jacobins de la rue Saint-Jacques'''
2 CONT '''Paris, City of Paris, Île-de-France, France'''
2 CONT Find a Grave Memorial ID: 86310038
2 CONT '''Nobility, son of''' Louis II and Anne de Auvergne. '''He was created Seigneur de Beaujeu. He was not married and died without heir.
2 CONT Family Members
2 CONT '''Parents'''
2 CONT Louis II de Bourbon
2 CONT 1337–1410
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne d'Auvergne
2 CONT 1351–1417
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Siblings'''
2 CONT Jean I de Bourbon
2 CONT 1381–1434
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/86310038/louis_de_bourbon
2 CONT ____
2 CONT Louis de Bourbon, called the Good (c.1337 – 1410), was the third Duke of Bourbon. He was also the Count of Clermont-en-Beauvaisis and Lord of Beaujeu.
2 CONT Life
2 CONT Louis was the son of Peter de Bourbon[1] and Isabella de Valois.[2] His mother was the sister to King Philip VI of France. He inherited the duchy after his father Duke Peter I died at the Battle of Poitiers in 1356.[3]
2 CONT In 1390, Louis launched the Barbary Crusade against the Hafsids of Tunis, in conjunction with the Genoese. Its objective was to suppress piracy based in the city of Mahdia, but the siege was unsuccessful.[4] Louis died at Montluço
2 CONC n in 1410, at the age of 73.
2 CONT Marriage and issue
2 CONT On 19 August 1371 Louis married Anne of Auvergne (1358–1417),[2] Countess of Forez and daughter of Beraud II, Dauphin of Auvergne, and his wife the Countess of Forez. They had:
2 CONT 1. Catherine of Bourbon (b. 1378),[2] d. young
2 CONT 2. John of Bourbon (1381–1434), Duke of Bourbon[1]
2 CONT '''3. Louis of Bourbon (1388 – 1404), Sieur de Beaujeu'''
2 CONT 4. Isabelle of Bourbon (1384 – aft. 1451)[2]
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_II,_Duke_of_Bourbon
2 CONT ___________
2 CONT 1.         LOUIS de Bourbon (4 Aug 1337-Château de Montluçon 19 Aug 1410, bur Priory of Souvigny).  He succeeded his father in 1356 as LOUIS II "le Bon" Duc de Bourbon, Comte de Clermont.  A charter dated 18 May 1370 refers to Lou
2 CONC is Duc d´Anjou as "curateur de Mgr Jean de Forez", and records the authorisation by Charles V King of France of the transfer of the former´s rights in the county of Forez to Louis II Duc de Bourbon, husband of Comte Jean´s niece A
2 CONC nne de Clermont[249].  Comte de Forez et de Roannais 15 May 1372, by right of his wife.  The testament of "Ysabeau de Valoys duchesse de Bourbon", dated 25 Jan 1379 (O.S.), appointed "notre...filz Loys duc de Bourbon" as her main 
2 CONC executor[250].  The testament of "Louis duc de Bourbonnais comte de Clermont et de Forez", dated to [Apr 1390], appoints “son fils Jean” as his heir “sous la tutelle de sa femme, et à défaut de celle-ci sous la tutelle du duc de B
2 CONC ourgogne, en considération du mariage projeté entre sondit fils et la fille du duc de Bourgogne”[251].  Head of the Council of Charles VI King of France.  Baron de Beaujeu et de Dombes 23 Jun 1400, by donation of Edouard Seigneur 
2 CONC de Beaujeu.  In return for his son inheriting the Duchy of Auvergne from his father-in-law Jean Duc de Berry, Duc Louis II agreed that the duchy of Bourbon and County of Clermont would revert to the French crown in case of extinct
2 CONC ion of his male line.  This change in status of the patrimony of the Bourbon family into a simple apanage was especially significant in view of the previous transmission of the old seigneurie de Bourbon through the female line on 
2 CONC two occasions.  The testament of "Louis duc de Bourbon", dated 24 Jan 1409 (N.S.), chose burial at Souvigny priory, appointed “son fils Jean” as his heir, required that “si le même Jean a second fils” he should receive “en apanage
2 CONC  la seigneurie de Beaujolais”, provided dowry for “sa fille Isabelle”, and appointed as executors “la duchesse sa femme, le duc de Berry, le comte de la Marche, le sire d’Albret”[252].  m (contract Montbrison 4 Jul 1368, in person
2 CONC  Ardes Jan 1370, Papal dispensation 15 Sep 1370) ANNE de Clermont Dame de Mercœur, daughter and heiress of BERAUD [II] Comte de Clermont [en Auvergne], Dauphin d'Auvergne & his first wife Jeanne de Forez dame d'Ussel (1358-Château
2 CONC  de Cleppé en Forez 22 Sep 1417, bur Priory of Souvigny).  Ctss de Forez.  "Domina Johanna de Borbonio, relicta…Guidonis comitis Forensis, comitissa Forensis" donated her rights in the county of Forez to "dominam Annam Dalphine du
2 CONC chissam Borbonii, filiam suam et…dominum Ludovicem ducem Borbonii eius nepotem, conjuges" by charter dated 5 Jul 1382[253].  Louis Duc de Bourbon transferred his rights in the county of Forez to his wife by charter dated 5 Jan 138
2 CONC 3[254].  Dauphine de Clermont 1400.  The testament of "Johanna de Bourbonio comitissa Forensis, filiaque…domini Ludovici ducis Borbonii, comitis Claromontis et Marchie, et domine Marie Heynaut, coniugem, relicta…domini Guidonis co
2 CONC mitis Forensis quondam", dated 13 Jun 1400, bequeathed property to "dominum ducem Bourboni, comitem Claromontensem et Forensem, et Annam Dalphinam, duchissam Bourbonii, nepotem et filiam meos", and founded an anniversary for "domi
2 CONC ne Margarite de Sabbadia…sororgie mee, quondam consortis…fratris mei domini Reynaudi de Foresio"[255].  The testament of "Anne Dauphine duchesse douairière de Bourbonnais", dated 19 Sep 1416, appointed “son fils Jean duc de Bourbo
2 CONC n” as her heir[256].  Louis & his wife had four children: 
2 CONT a)         JEAN de Bourbon (Mar 1381-in prison London 5 Jan 1434, bur Priory of Souvigny).  The testament of "Louis duc de Bourbonnais comte de Clermont et de Forez", dated to [Apr 1390], appoints “son fils Jean” as his heir “sous
2 CONC  la tutelle de sa femme, et à défaut de celle-ci sous la tutelle du duc de Bourgogne, en considération du mariage projeté entre sondit fils et la fille du duc de Bourgogne”[257].  Comte de Clermont [Jul 1399].  Chamberlain of Fran
2 CONC ce 18 Mar 1408.  He succeeded his father in 1410 as JEAN I Duc de Bourbon.   
2 CONT -        see below. 
2 CONT b)         ISABELLE de Bourbon (-after 1451).  The testament of "Louis duc de Bourbon", dated 24 Jan 1409 (N.S.), provided dowry for “sa fille Isabelle”[258].  She became a nun at the Priory of Poissy.   Betrothed (1400) to ERIK V
2 CONC II King of Denmark and Norway, son of WARTISLAW VII Duke of Pomerania in Hinterpommern & his wife Marie von Mecklenburg-Schwerin ([1381]-Rügenwalde 1459 [after 4 Apr], bur Rügenwalde, Marienkirche). 
2 CONT c)         CATHERINE de Bourbon (-young).  La Mure names “la cadette...Catherine de Bourbon, elle mourut en jeunesse” but does not cite the source on which this information is based[259].  Père Anselme lists her as the older daugh
2 CONC ter[260]. 
2 CONT '''d)         LOUIS de Bourbon ([1387]-Paris, Hôtel de Bourbon 12 Sep 1404, bur Paris, église des Jacobins).  The chapter of Notre-Dame de Moulins agreed 22 Jun 1409 to celebrate "un obit pour le repos de l’âme de Louis fils du du
2 CONC c de Bourbon, décédé le 12 septembre" '''[261]. 
2 CONT Louis had [three] illegitimate children by unknown mistresses: 
2 CONT e)         HECTOR bâtard de Bourbon ([1390?]-Soissons 11 May 1414).  According to Père Anselme, Hector was born “d’une fille de qualité”[262].  Fieyre suggests his birth “v. 1390?” (no source cited)[263].  "Jean duc de Bourbonnais
2 CONC  comte de Clermont" granted a pension to “Hector bâtard de Bourbon”, in exchange for “des terres de Chaveroche en Bourbonnais et de la Hérelle en Beauvoisis” granted to him by “le feu duc leur père commun”, by charter dated [befor
2 CONC e 21] Aug 1413[264].  Seigneur de Rochefort et de Dampierre-en-Champagne.  He died from wounds received at the siege of Soissons[265]. 
2 CONT f)           PERCEVAL bâtard de Bourbon (-after 6 Sep 1415).   Père Anselme says that “Perceval de Bourbon qualifié chevalier le 6 septembre 1415 pouvoit être bâtard du duc Louis II”[266], implying uncertainty about his parentage.
2 CONC   Fieyre indicates his “illégiitimité avérée ; filiation déduite…Par les armoiries et l’emblématique du sceau”[267].  The Chronique de Jean le Févre Seigneur de Saint-Remy records, in 1414, that “l’un des bastart de Bourbon, après
2 CONC  la mort de son frère messire Hector” was called to the siege of Arras, a later passage recording that “le bastard de Bourbon, joane enfant” was appointed by “le duc de Bourbon…chief des quatre Franchois” and recounting his milita
2 CONC rry exploits[268].  Fieyre suggests, in relation to these passages, that “Il est possible que la chronique de Jean Le Fèvre par[le] de lui dès 1414”[269]. 
2 CONT g)         [PIERRE bâtard de Bourbon .  Van Kerrebrouck records his parentage[270].  He cites no source, but presumably his report is based on a comment by Dussieux that “Le P. Labbé cite trois autres bâtards de Louis II, savoir :
2 CONC  Jacques de Bourbon, seigneur de Thury en 1419; Pierre de Bourbon, chevalier; Frère Jacques de Bourbon, de l’ordre des Célestins, en 1422”[271].  It seems likely that, in the case of “Jacques de Bourbon, seigneur de Thury en 1419…
2 CONC [et] Frère Jacques de Bourbon, de l’ordre des Célestins, en 1422””, they refer to the same person whom Labbé confused with Jacques de Bourbon, Seigneur d’Argies et de Préaux, Baron de Thury (son of Jacques de Clermont Comte de la 
2 CONC Marche et de Ponthieu, see above), who, according to Père Anselme, after his wife’s death “prit l’habit religieux au monastere des Celestins d’Ambert dans la forêt d'Orléans”[272].  Presumably some confusion is also possible in th
2 CONC e case of Pierre.  Père Anselme does not name Pierre[273].  It is suggested that his existence be treated with caution.] 
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/BOURBON.htm#LouisIIDucdied1410
2 CONT ______
