1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eleanor_of_Aragon,_Queen_of_Cyprus
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor of Aragon (1333 – 26 December 1417) was Queen consort of Cyprus as the wife of King Peter I of Cyprus. She was a member of the House of Barcelona as the daughter of Peter of Aragon and his wife Joan of Foix.
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen of Cyprus
2 CONT
2 CONT To enhance his kingdom's political and economic power in the Mediterranean, King Peter IV of Aragon arranged a marriage in 1353 between his cousin Eleanor and Peter I of Cyprus. By this marriage Eleanor became Queen of Cyprus and 
2 CONC titular Queen of Jerusalem and Armenia.
2 CONT
2 CONT The reign of Eleanor's husband was not a windfall for Cyprus. Her husband kept mistresses, which she was unable to endure. In 1366, when Peter went off on a crusade against Alexandria, he made Eleanor regent of Cyprus.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the return of Peter I from a long trip to Europe, Eleonor was accused of having committed adultery with John of Morf, the titular of Edessa. The court convened and subsequently dismissed the charges. Then Peter tyranisierte 
2 CONC preferred by Eleanor nobles, including his own brothers. On 17 January 1369, he was eventually murdered in the arms of a lover, perhaps on the orders of his brothers John and James.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter and Eleanor had the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT  Peter II of Cyprus (c. 1357 – 1382), succeeded him as King of Cyprus and Jerusalem
2 CONT
2 CONT  Margaret or Maria (ca. 1360 – ca. 1397), once engaged to Carlo Visconti and married in 1385 to her cousin James of Lusignan (d. 1395/1397), titular Count of Tripoli, grandson of John of Lusignan and wife Alice of Ibelin, and had 
2 CONC issue
2 CONT
2 CONT  Eschive (d. before 1369), died young. It is possible that she was the daughter of Peter's first wife, Eschive de Montfort rather than Eleanor 
2 CONT
2 CONT On behalf of her son, Eleanor was again regent, together with the brothers of the king, John and James. She stood first in Cyprus to prevent the throne of her son from being usurped.
2 CONT
2 CONT In a secret message, she invited the Genoese to a de facto invasion of Cyprus, to take revenge on her brothers-in-law. With the help of the betrayal of John Morf, they were conquered by the Genoese in 1373 and captured James. Elea
2 CONC nor led the assassination of John in 1374.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1378, Peter II married Valentina Visconti, daughter of Barnabò Visconti. The first choice of his marriage would have been to a daughter of John V Palaiologos; this suggestion was rejected for political reasons, since the Latins
2 CONC  did not encourage the marriage of Peter to a Greek princess. The justification that was given to the Palaiologos messengers was that the king was busy with the dangers that threatened Cyprus because of the Genoese invasion of the
2 CONC  island.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is known that Eleanor did not get along with her daughter-in-law Valentina due to being involved in many issues and scandals.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1381, the now 44-year-old Eleanor was forced to return to Catalonia, in order to prevent more problems with Valentina.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lady of Valls
2 CONT
2 CONT After her return, her cousin, Peter IV of Aragon, entrusted Eleanor with the city of Valls. She was co-ruler with the archbishop of Tarragona.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor moved into the archbishop's palace in Valls. This changed within a short time into a sovereign court with numerous officials and minions; some had accompanied her from Cyprus.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was necessary in Valls that a tax had to be paid on the import of wine. The favourites of the Queen, however, insisted that they were exempt from these taxes. This led to a revolt, with casualties on both sides. There was no ch
2 CONC oice but for her friends to pay the taxes.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the death of King Peter IV, power was transferred to his son and successor, King John I. The clashes between the population and the Queen Dowager of Cyprus, however, still continued. For alleged insults against the citizens,
2 CONC  they broke into the palace and killed John, brother and butler to Eleanor; he was murdered before Eleanor's eyes. Terrified, Eleanor fled to territory of her brother. After twelve years of living in Valls, Eleanor never returned.
2 CONT
2 CONT She died on 26 December 1416 in the Castle of Falsetto, Barcelona.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancestors
2 CONT
