1 OCCU Prince d'Achaïe & de Morée, Comte de Piémont, titular Pr of Achaia, sovereign Prince of Piedmont (1334-67) Prince de Andravida
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_of_Piedmont
2 CONT
2 CONT James of Piedmont
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT James (16 January 1315[1] – 17 May 1367[2]) was the Lord of Piedmont from 1334 to his death. He was the eldest son of Philip I and Catherine de la Tour du Pin. While his father had abandoned his claim to the Principality of Achaea
2 CONC  in 1307, James continued to use the princely title and even passed it on to his successors.
2 CONT
2 CONT James opposed Robert of Taranto in Achaea in the 1340s. He began a war with Amadeus VI of Savoy, but was captured at Pinerolo and his territories confiscated. A treaty of 2 July 1362 returned them, however. James died at Pinerolo 
2 CONC a few years later.
2 CONT
2 CONT James married his first wife Beatrice (died 1339), daughter of Rinaldo II d'Este, in 1338. They had one son, Philip II. James remarried on 9 June 1339 to Sibyl (died 1361), daughter of Raymond II of Baux but had no children by her
2 CONC . Widowered for a second time, James remarried on 16 July 1362 to Margaret (1346 – 1402), daughter of Edward I of Beaujeu, They had two sons: Amadeus and Louis.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. ^ Or 6 January.
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. ^ Or 14 May.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Foundation for Medieval Genealogy: Savoy.
2 CONT
