1 OCCU Seigneur de Lusignan, Couhé, and Château-Larcher and Count of La Marche, SIRE OF LUSIGNAN, Comte, de la Marche, Sieur, de Lusignan, Chevalier, Croisé, Seigneur
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/AQUITAINE%20NOBILITY.htm#GuyKingJerusalemdied1194
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Hugh VIII the Old of Lusignan or Hugh III of La Marche or Hugues VIII le Vieux de Lusignan was the eldest son of Hugh VII and of Sarrasine or Saracena de Lezay. He became Seigneur de Lusignan, Couhé, and Château-Larcher and Count 
2 CONC of La Marche on his father's death in 1151. Born in Poitou, 1106-1110 or some time after 1125, he died in Holy Land in 1165 or 1171.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married in 1140/1141 Bourgogne or Burgondie de Rancon, Dame de Fontenay, daughter of Geoffroi or Geoffroy de Rancon, Seigneur de Taillebourg and wife Fossefie=Falsifie, Dame de Moncontour, by whom he also became Seigneur de Fon
2 CONC tenay: she died in April 11, 1169. In 1163 or 1164 he went on pilgrimage and on crusade to the Holy Land and participated in the Battle of Harim, where he was taken prisoner.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Harim
2 CONT
2 CONT His children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugues de Lusignan, Co-Seigneur de Lusignan in 1164 (c. 1141 - 1169), married before 1162 Orengarde N, who died in 1169, leaving an infant son Hugues who was to become Hugh IX of Lusignan, and an infant son Raoul who was to become
2 CONC  Raoul I de Lusignan  Robert de Lusignan, died young c. 1150  Geoffrey or Geoffroy I de Lusignan (bef. 1150 – May, 1224), Seigneur of Moncontour and Seigneur de Soubise, Seigneur de Vouvent, de Mervent by first marriage, Count of 
2 CONC Jaffa and Ascalon on July 28, 1191 (he relinquished these titles upon his return from the Holy Land in 1193), who fought in the Siege of Acre. According to the Chronicle of Ernoul, when told that Guy had become king of Jerusalem G
2 CONC eoffrey said: "He'll be God next!". Married firstly before 1200 Eustache de Chabot, Dame de Vouvent et Dame de Mervent (d. after 1200), and secondly c. 1202 Humberge de Limoges, daughter of Aimar VI, Vicomte de Limoges and wife Sa
2 CONC rra de Cornouailles, and had one son by each marriage:  Geoffroy II de Lusignan, Seigneur de Vouvent, de Mevent et de Montcontour, married 1223 Clémence de Chattellerault, Dame de Chattellerault, without issue  Guillaume de Lusign
2 CONC an, married c. 1226 Marguerite de Mauléon, and had one daughter:  Valence de Lusignan, married aft. 1247 Hugues III de Parthenay (d. 1271)  Pierre de Lusignan (bef. 1155 – aft. December, 1174), a "Peter of Lusignan" witnessed a ch
2 CONC arter in Antioch in 1174, but is otherwise not documented. He died probably as a Priest.  Amalric II & I de Lusignan, born about 1145, died 1205. He succeeded his younger brother Guy as ruler of Cyprus; later he was crowned King o
2 CONC f Cyprus, the first of the Lusignan dynasty, and eventually also became King of Jerusalem.  Guy I & I de Lusignan, died 1194. He was regent and afterwards King of Jerusalem. After the loss of Jerusalem he became Lord of Cyprus.  G
2 CONC uillaume de Lusignan or de Valence, born after 1163, betrothed to Beatrice de Courtenay, daughter of Joscelin III of Edessa, in 1186. The marriage does not seem to have taken place. He died before 1208.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Hugh VIII of Lusignan From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Hugh VIII the Old of Lusignan or Hugh III of La Marche or Hugues VIII le Vieux de Lusignan was the eldest son of Hugh VII and of Sarrasine or Saracena de Lezay. He became Seigneur de Lusignan, Couhé, and Château-Larcher and Count 
2 CONC of La Marche on his father's death in 1151. Born in Poitou, 1106-1110 or some time after 1125, he died in Holy Land in 1165 or 1171. He married in 1140/1141 Bourgogne or Burgondie de Rancon, Dame de Fontenay, daughter of Geoffroi 
2 CONC or Geoffroy de Rancon, Seigneur de Taillebourg and wife Fossefie=Falsifie, Dame de Moncontour, by whom he also became Seigneur de Fontenay: she died in April 11, 1169. In 1163 or 1164 he went on pilgrimage and on crusade to the Ho
2 CONC ly Land and participated in the Battle of Harim, where he was taken prisoner. His children were: Hugues de Lusignan, Co-Seigneur de Lusignan in 1164 (c. 1141 - 1169), married before 1162 Orengarde N, who died in 1169, leaving an i
2 CONC nfant son Hugues who was to become Hugh IX of Lusignan, and an infant son Raoul who was to become Raoul I de Lusignan Robert de Lusignan, died young c. 1150 Geoffrey or Geoffroy I de Lusignan (bef. 1150 – May, 1224), Seigneur of M
2 CONC oncontour and Seigneur de Soubise, Seigneur de Vouvent, de Mervent by first marriage, Count of Jaffa and Ascalon on July 28, 1191 (he relinquished these titles upon his return from the Holy Land in 1193), who fought in the Siege o
2 CONC f Acre. According to the Chronicle of Ernoul, when told that Guy had become king of Jerusalem Geoffrey said: "He'll be God next!". Married firstly before 1200 Eustache de Chabot, Dame de Vouvent et Dame de Mervent (d. after 1200),
2 CONC  and secondly c. 1202 Humberge de Limoges, daughter of Aimar VI, Vicomte de Limoges and wife Sarra de Cornouailles, and had one son by each marriage: Geoffroy II de Lusignan, Seigneur de Vouvent, de Mevent et de Montcontour, marri
2 CONC ed 1223 Clémence de Chattellerault, Dame de Chattellerault, without issue Guillaume de Lusignan, married c. 1226 Marguerite de Mauléon, and had one daughter: Valence de Lusignan, married aft. 1247 Hugues III de Parthenay (d. 1271)
2 CONC  Pierre de Lusignan (bef. 1155 – aft. December, 1174), a "Peter of Lusignan" witnessed a charter in Antioch in 1174, but is otherwise not documented. He died probably as a Priest. Amalric II & I de Lusignan, born about 1145, died 
2 CONC 1205. He succeeded his younger brother Guy as ruler of Cyprus; later he was crowned King of Cyprus, the first of the Lusignan dynasty, and eventually also became King of Jerusalem. Guy I & I de Lusignan, died 1194. He was regent a
2 CONC nd afterwards King of Jerusalem. After the loss of Jerusalem he became Lord of Cyprus. Guillaume de Lusignan or de Valence, born after 1163, betrothed to Beatrice de Courtenay, daughter of Joscelin III of Edessa, in 1186. The marr
2 CONC iage does not seem to have taken place. He died before 1208.
2 CONT --------------------
