2 CONT 1.  John, King of England, conspired unsuccessfully with Philip II ofFran ce to supplant his older brother, Richard, as King of England whenRicha rd left on the Third Crusade.  On Richard's death, John ascended tothe th rone to th
2 CONC e exclusion of his nephew Arthur I of Brittany, who,aided by Ph ilip II of France, began a revolt in France.  John's reignwas 1199 - 121 6.  Burkes Peerage records 1160 as date of birth.
2 CONT Although Arthur was captured (1202), John lost many of his Frenchpossessi ons to Philip.
2 CONT John objected to Pope Innocent III's choice for archbishop of Canterburya nd the pope placed England under an interdict March 24, 1208 forbiddingt he clergy to administer sacraments.  John's refusal to accept the newarch bishop le
2 CONC d to his excommunication in 1209.  He regained papal favorby sur rendering his kingdom to Pope Innocent III but received it back asa pap al fief.
2 CONT The Magna Carta, reaffirming human rights, signed by John at Runnyme dein mid-June, 1215 limited the power of the English monarchy.  Feudal barons supported by Scotland's new king Alexander II met with John betweenS taines and Win
2 CONC dsor, exacted major concessions reaffirming traditionalfeud al privileges contained in the accession charter signed by Henry I acentu ry before and compelled John to sethis seal to the Magna Carta.John imme diately appealed to Pop
2 CONC e Innocent III, who issued a bullannulling the cha rter.  John imported French mercenaries to fight thebarons, but the Mag na Carta remained the basis of English feudal justice.
2 CONT John came down with dysentery in October, 1216 after crushing resistan cein the north. He crossed the Wash and reached Newark Castle but died there October 19, 1216 at age 48. The king was succeeded by his 9-year-olds on, John, wh
2 CONC o reigned until 1272 as Henry III; the earl of PembrokeWilli am Marshal served as regent, and the moderate party took control,ending t he need for opposition to royal authority.
2 CONT
