2 CONT Joan of England, Queen of Scots
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan of England
2 CONT
2 CONT Queen consort of Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT Tenure 	21 June 1221 – 4 March 1238
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse 	Alexander II, King of Scots
2 CONT
2 CONT House 	House of Plantagenet (by birth)
2 CONT
2 CONT House of Dunkeld (by marriage)
2 CONT
2 CONT Father 	John Lackland, King of England
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother 	Isabella of Angoulême
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 	22 July 1210(1210-07-22)
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 	4 March 1238 (aged 27)
2 CONT
2 CONT Havering-atte-Bower, London
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial 	Tarant Crawford Abbey, Dorset
2 CONT
2 CONT     For the wife of David II of Scotland see Joan of The Tower.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan of England (22 July 1210 – 4 March 1238) was Queen consort of Scotland from 1221 until 1238.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan was the eldest legitimate daughter and third child of King John of England and Countess Isabella of Angoulême.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan was brought up in the court of Hugh X of Lusignan who was promised to her in marriage from an early age, as compensation for him being jilted by her mother Isabella of Angoulême, however on the death of John of England, Isabe
2 CONC lla decided she should marry him herself and Joan was sent back to England, where negotiations for her hand with Alexander II of Scotland were taking place.
2 CONT
2 CONT She and Alexander married on 21 June 1221, at York Minster[1]. Alexander was twenty-three. Joan was ten, almost eleven. They had no children. Joan died in her brother's arms at Havering-atte-Bower in 1238, and was buried at Tarran
2 CONC t Crawford Abbey in Dorset[2].
2 CONT
2 CONT Nothing now remains of this church; the last mention of it is before the Reformation.l
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. ^ Agnes Mure Mackenzie, The Foundations of Scotland (1957), p. 251.
2 CONT
2 CONT    2. ^ Mackenzie, p. 260.
2 CONT
2 CONT Preceded by
2 CONT
2 CONT Ermengarde de Beaumont 	Queen consort of Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT 1221–1238 	Succeeded by
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie de Coucy
2 CONT
2 CONT This page was last modified on 13 June 2010 at 17:36.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan, Princess of Wales and Lady of Snowdon, (c. 1191 – 2 February 1237) was the wife of Llywelyn the Great, Prince of Wales and Gwynedd and effective ruler of most of Wales.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents [hide]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 Adultery with William de Braose
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 Fiction
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 References
2 CONT
2 CONT 6 Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT 7 External links
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Early life
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan was a natural daughter of King John of England. She should not be confused with her half-sister Joan, Queen Consort of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Little is known about her early life. Her mother's name is known only from Joan's obituary in the Tewkesbury Annals, where she is called "Regina Clementina" (Queen Clemence); there is no evidence that her mother was in fact of roy
2 CONC al blood. Joan seems to have spent part of her childhood in France, as King John had her brought to the Kingdom of England from Normandy in December 1203 in preparation for her wedding to prince Llywelyn ab Iorwerth.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Marriage
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan married Llywelyn the Great between December 1203 and October 1204. She and Llywelyn had at least two children together:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.Elen ferch Llywelyn (Helen or Ellen) (1207-1253), married (1) John the Scot, Earl of Chester and (2) Robert II de Quincy
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.Dafydd ap Llywelyn (c. 1215-1246) married Isabella de Braose, died at Garth Celyn, Aber Garth Celyn, (Aber).
2 CONT
2 CONT Some of Llywelyn's other recorded children may also have been Joan's:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.Gwladus Ddu (1206-1251), married (1) Reginald de Braose and (2) Ralph de Mortimer.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.Susanna, who was sent to England as a hostage in 1228.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3.Margaret, who married Sir John de Braose, the grandson of William de Braose, 4th Lord of Bramber and had issue.
2 CONT
2 CONT In April 1226 Joan obtained a papal decree from Pope Honorius III, declaring her legitimate on the basis that her parents had not been married to others at the time of her birth, but without giving her a claim to the English thron
2 CONC e.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Adultery with William de Braose
2 CONT
2 CONT At Easter 1230, William de Braose, 10th Baron Abergavenny, who was Llywelyn's prisoner at the time, was discovered with Joan in Llywelyn's bedchamber. William de Braose was hanged at Aber Garth Celyn on 2 May 1230; the place was k
2 CONC nown as 'Gwern y Grog' and the incident remembered down the generations by the local community. A recent suggestion that the execution might have taken place at Crogen near Bala rests on the suggestion that 'Crogen' and 'Crokein' 
2 CONC are one and the same: there is however no further eveidence in the area to lend this substance.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan was placed under house arrest for twelve months after the incident. She was then, according to the Chronicle of Chester, forgiven by Llywelyn, and restored to favour. She may have given birth to a daughter early in 1231.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan was never called Princess of Wales, but, in Welsh, "Lady of Wales". She died at the royal home, Garth Celyn, Aber Garth Celyn, on the north coast of Gwynedd in 1237. Llywelyn's great grief at her death is recorded; he founded
2 CONC  a Franciscan friary on the seashore at Llanfaes, opposite the royal home, in her honour. The friary was consecrated in 1240, shortly before Llywelyn died. It was destroyed in 1537 by Henry VIII of England during the Dissolution o
2 CONC f the Monasteries.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan's stone coffin can be seen in Beaumaris parish church, Anglesey. Above the empty coffin is a slate panel inscribed: "This plain sarcophagus, (once dignified as having contained the remains of JOAN, daughter of King JOHN, and 
2 CONC consort of LLEWELYN ap IOWERTH, Prince of North Wales, who died in the year 1237), having been conveyed from the Friary of Llanfaes, and alas, used for many years as a horsewatering trough, was rescued from such an indignity and p
2 CONC laced here for preseravation as well as to excite serious meditation on the transitory nature of all sublunary distinctions. By THOMAS JAMES WARREN BULKELEY, Viscount BULKELEY, Oct 1808"
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Fiction
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan and her affair with William de Braose is the subject of Saunders Lewis's Welsh verse play Siwan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edith Pargeter's novel The Green Branch.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sharon Kay Penman's novel Here Be Dragons.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] References
2 CONT
