2 CONT
2 CONT -------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan, Lady of Wales, or Joan of England (died March 1236) was the wife ofLlywelyn the Great, Prince of Gwynedd and effective ruler of most ofWales.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan was an illegitimate daughter of King John of England and a womannamed Clemence [AKA Clemantina, the wife of Henry Pinel]. She should notbe confused with her legitimate half-sister Joan, Queen of Scotland.Little is known about
2 CONC  her early life; she was evidently born before herfather, King John, married his first wife in 1189. Her mother's name isknown only from Joan's obituary in the Tewkesbury Annals, where she ismysteriously called "Regina Clementina"
2 CONC  (Queen Clemence). [Note: Someothers assert her mother was Agatha de Ferrers]. Joan seems to have spenther childhood in France, as King John had her brought to England fromNormandy for her wedding in December 1203.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan married Llywelyn the Great between December 1203 and October 1204.She and Llywelyn had at least two children together:
2 CONT
2 CONT Elen ferch Llywelyn (died 1253), married (1) John the Scot, Earl ofChester and (2) Robert de Quincy
2 CONT Dafydd ap Llywelyn (after 1211-1246)
2 CONT Some of Llywelyn's other recorded children may also have been Joan's:
2 CONT
2 CONT Gwladys Ddu (died 1251), married (1) Reginald de Braose and (2) Ralph deMortimer
2 CONT Susanna, who was sent to England as a hostage in 1228 .
2 CONT
2 CONT In April 1226 Joan obtained a papal decree from Pope Honorius III,declaring her legitimate on the basis that her parents had been unmarriedat the time of her birth, but without giving her a claim to the Englishthrone. In 1230, Joa
2 CONC n was alleged to have committed adultery with Williamde Braose, Lord of Abergavenny, a relation by marriage who was Llywelyn'snominal prisoner at the time. Some sources say that the couple werediscovered together in Llywelyn's bed
2 CONC chamger. William de Braose washanged, and Joan herself was imprisoned for some time before Llywelynaccepted her back as his wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan was never called Princess of Wales, but she was sometimes known as"Lady of Wales". Llywelyn's great grief at her death is recorded. She wasburied at the priory of Llanfaes near Beaumaris, and her stone coffin canbe seen in Be
2 CONC aumaris parish church.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fiction
2 CONT This princess Joan and her affair with William de Braose was the subjectof Saunders Lewis's Welsh verse play Siwan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joan appears as a main character in Sharon Kay Penman's historical novelHere Be Dragons.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT Rotuli Litterarum Clausarum in Turri Londinensi I, p. 12.
2 CONT Luard, Henry. Annales Monastici 1, 1864
2 CONT Tewkesbury Annals
2 CONT Chronicle of Ystrad Fflur
