2 CONT Preceded by Gerberga 	Countess of Provence 1112–1127 	Succeeded by Berenguer Ramon I This page was last modified on 11 January 2010 at 22:35
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Douce I (also Dulcia or Dolça, called "of Rouergue" or "of Gévaudan") (c. 1090–1127) was the daughter of Gilbert I of Gévaudan and Gerberga of Provence and wife of Ramon Berenguer III, Count of Barcelona. In 1112, she inherited th
2 CONC e county of Provence through her mother. She married Ramon Berenguer at Arles on 3 February that year.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1113, Douce ceded her rights in Provence, Gévaudan, and the viscounty of Millau to her husband. According to a once prevailing opinion, "Provençal troubadours ... entered Catalonia at the time" and even the Catalan language was
2 CONC  imported from Provence.[1] According to nationalist historians it was the beginning of l'engrandiment occitànic (the Occitan aggrandisement): a great scheme to unite various lands on both sides of the Pyrenees.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT In reality the marriage gave the House of Barcelona extensive interests in Occitania and put it in conflict with the Counts of Toulouse, with whom a partition of Provence was signed in 1125, shortly before Douce's death. Her death
2 CONC  inaugurated a period of instability in Provence. A cadet branch of the House of Barcelona was set up to rule, but a disputed succession opened up the Baussenque Wars (1144–1162), which terminated in Provençal victory. Douce and R
2 CONC amon Berenguer's descendants continued to rule Provence until the death of Beatrice of Provence in 1267.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her children with Ramon Berenguer were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Almodis, married Ponce de Cervera  Berenguela (1116–1149), married Alfonso VII of Castile  Ramon Berenguer (1113–1162), Count of Barcelona  Berenguer Ramon (c. 1115–1144), Count of Provence  Bernard, died young 
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] References ^ Henry John Chaytor (1933), A History of Aragon and Catalonia (London: Methuen), 63–4, who shows both views to be questionable.  ^ Thomas N. Bisson (1984), "The Rise of Catalonia: Identity, Power, and Ideology i
2 CONC n a Twelfth-Century Society," Annales: Economies, Sociétés, Civilisations, xxxix, translated in Medieval France and her Pyrenean Neighbours: Studies in Early Institutional History (London: Hambledon, 1989), 179.  Preceded by Gerbe
2 CONC rga Countess of Provence 1112–1127 Succeeded by Berenguer Ramon I 
2 CONT
2 CONT Retrieved from "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Douce_I,_Countess_of_Provence"
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT In 1112, Dolça I (also Dulcia or Douce, called "of Rouergue" or "of Gévaudan") (c. 1090–1127) inherited the county of Provence through her mother. She married Ramon Berenguer at Arles on 3 February that year.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1113, Douce ceded her rights in Provence, Gévaudan, and the viscounty of Millau to her husband. By her marriage, she had already linked the history of Provence with that of Catalonia. Her death in 1127 inaugurated a period of i
2 CONC nstability in Provence, which did not terminate until the Baussenque Wars (1144-1162), in which the Provençals defeated the Catalans.
2 CONT
2 CONT See http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Douce_of_Provence for more information.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Dulce Aldonza de Milhaud, Condesa de Provenza nació hacia 1095 en Gévaudan, Essone, Francia. Murió entre 1127 y 1130. Casó, el 3-II-1111/1112, con Ramón Berenguer III, Conde de Barcelona. Tuvieron por hija a Berenguela de Barcelon
2 CONC a (c.1116), que casó con Alfonso VII de Castilla.
2 CONT --------------------
