1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Louis de Chalon never married, but he left an illegitimate son: Jean de Chalon. He is indeed attested in several posthumous documents after his father's death. He is then declared the son of Louis de Chalon and Fra
2 CONC nçoise d'Amboise, one of the last daughters of Pierre d'Amboise, Lord of Chaumont, and Anne de Bueil.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis de Chalon never married, although by the time of his death he had fathered a son with Françoise d'Amboise. The reason they did not marry is unknown: it may have been Louis's premature death or a problem of consanguinity. Fro
2 CONC m this union was born at least one son, Jean, considered illegitimate because his parents never married, born around 1467.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean came under the protection of his great-uncle Charles d'Armagnac, who granted him several of his fiefs. Thus, Jean de Chalon became Baron of Roumégoux.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean-Pierre Beau, História da Casa de Chalon , vol.  2,2000.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://books.google.com.br/books?hl=pt-BR&lr=&id=SlMk_EqDvikC&oi=fnd&pg=PA5&dq=Jean-Pierre+Beau,+Hist%C3%B3ria+da+Casa+de+Chalon+,+vol.++2,2000.&ots=eib929Leqz&sig=I86e2ZRn7PCGE5cebj72_E7bBJA&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q&f=false
