1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Jean (John) de Bourbon, Duke of Bourbon (1426 – 1 April 1488), sometimes referred to as John the Good and The Scourge of the English, was a son''' of Charles I of Bourbon and Agnes of Burgundy. '''He was Duke of
2 CONC  Bourbon and Auvergne from 1456 to his death'''.[1]
2 CONT Life
2 CONT '''John earned his nicknames "John the Good" and "The Scourge of the English" for his efforts in helping drive out the English from France'''.
2 CONT '''He was made constable of France in 1483 by his brother Peter and sister-in-law Anne, to neutralize him as a threat to their regency'''.[citation needed]
2 CONT '''In an effort to win discontented nobles back to his side, Louis XI of France made great efforts to give out magnificent gifts to certain individuals; John was a recipient of these overtures. According to contemporary chronicles
2 CONC , the King received John in Paris with "honours, caresses, pardon, and gifts; everything was lavished upon him".'''
2 CONT '''John is notable for making three brilliant alliances but leaving no legitimate issue'''.
2 CONT '''First marriage'''
2 CONT '''In 1447, his father, the Duke of Bourbon, had John married to a daughter of Charles VII, King of France, Joan of Valois.[2] They were duly married at the Château de Moulins. They had no surviving issue'''.
2 CONT '''Second marriage'''
2 CONT '''In 1484 at St. Cloud to Catherine of Armagnac''', daughter of Jacques of Armagnac, Duke of Nemours, who died in 1487 while giving birth to:
2 CONT *John of Bourbon (Moulins, 1487 - 1487), styled Count of Clermont
2 CONT '''Third marriage'''
2 CONT '''In 1487 he married Jeanne of Bourbon-Vendôme''', daughter of John of Bourbon, Count of Vendôme (from a cadet branch of the House of Bourbon), '''by whom he had one son''':
2 CONT *Louis of Bourbon (1488 - 1488), styled Count of Clermont
2 CONT '''Illegitimate issue'''
2 CONT '''By Louise of Albret''', daughter of Jean I d'Albret (- 8 September 1494)[a]:
2 CONT *Charles, Bastard of Bourbon (- 1502), Viscount of Lavedan jure uxoris, married before 1462 Louise du Lion (- aft. 25 February 1505), Viscountess of Lavedan, and had issue, four sons
2 CONT '''By Marguerite de Brunant''':
2 CONT *Mathieu, the Great Bastard of Bourbon[3] (- Château de Chambrou-en-Forez, 19 August 1505), Lord of Botheon and Lord and Baron of Roche-en-Régnier, unmarried and without issue
2 CONT '''By unknown women''':
2 CONT *Hector, Bastard of Bourbon (- 1502, bur. Toulouse), 15th Archbishop of Toulouse (1491 - 1502), 17th Bishop of Lavaur (1497 - 1500)
2 CONT *Peter, Bastard of Bourbon, died young, unmarried and without issue
2 CONT *Marie, Bastard of Bourbon (- 22 July 1482), married at the Château de Beseneins-en-Dombes in 1470 Jacques de Sainte Colombe, Lord of Thil
2 CONT *Marguerite, Bastard of Bourbon (1445 - 1482), legitimized in 1464, married in Moulins in 1462 Jean de Ferrières (- 1497)
2 CONT Death and aftermath
2 CONT '''John died in 1488 at the Château de Moulins and was succeeded by his younger brother''' Charles. However, this succession was strongly contested due to the political strength of Peter and Anne. '''Within a span of days, Charles
2 CONC  was forced to renounce his claims to the Bourbon lands to Peter in exchange for a financial settlement'''.
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_II,_Duke_of_Bourbon
2 CONT ________
2 CONT '''Jean II de Bourbon, Duc de Bourbon'''1 
2 CONT M, #113679, '''b. 1426, d. 1488'''
2 CONT Last Edited=30 Jun 2020
2 CONT Consanguinity Index=2.64%
2 CONT '''     Jean II de Bourbon, Duc de Bourbon was born in 1426.1 He was the son''' of Charles I de Bourbon, Duc de Bourbon and Agnes de Bourgogne.1 '''He married Jeanne de Bourbon''', daughter of Jean II de Bourbon, Comte de Vendôme 
2 CONC and Isabel de Beauveau, '''in 1487.2 He died in 1488.'''1
2 CONT '''     He gained the title of Duc de Bourbon.'''1
2 CONT '''Child of Jean II de Bourbon, Duc de Bourbon and Jeanne de Bourbon'''
2 CONT 1.Louis de Bourbon, Comte de Clermont2 b. 1488, d. 1488
2 CONT Citations
