2 CONT As Duchess, Anne fiercely defended the independence of her Duchy. She arranged the marriage of her daughter, Claude, to Charles of Luxembourg in 1501, to reinforce the Franco-Spanish alliance and ensure French success in the Itali
2 CONC an Wars; however, Louis broke off the marriage when it became likely that Anne would not produce a male heir. Instead, Louis arranged a marriage between Claude and the heir to the French throne, Francis of Angouleme. Anne, determi
2 CONC ned to maintain Breton independence, refused until death to sanction the marriage, pushing instead for Claude to marry Charles, or for the Duchy to be inherited by her other daughter, Renee. The marriage of Claude and Francis even
2 CONC tually took place in the year following Anne's death.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Death
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne failed to survive the winter of 1513-1514, dying of a kidney-stone attack at the Chateau of Blois. She was buried in the necropolis of Saint Denis. Her funeral was of exceptional length, lasting 40 days, and inspiring all fut
2 CONC ure French royal funerals until the 18th century. The Requiem for Anne was probably composed by the famous composer Johannes Prioris[2].
2 CONT
2 CONT Reliquary of Anne of Brittany.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to her will, her heart was placed in a raised enamel gold reliquary, then transported to Nantes to be deposited, on 19 March 1514, in the vault of the Carmelite friars, in the tomb made for her parents, later being trans
2 CONC ferred to the Saint-Pierre cathedral. The reliquary of the heart of the Anne, Duchess of Brittany is a box oval, bivalvular, made of a sheet of gold pushed back and guilloched, articulated by a hinge, broadside of a gold cordelièr
2 CONC e and topped by a crown of lily and clover. It is inscribed as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT     En ce petit vaisseau
2 CONT
2 CONT     De fin or pur et munde
2 CONT
2 CONT     Repose ung plus grand cueur
2 CONT
2 CONT     Que oncque dame eut au munde
2 CONT
2 CONT     Anne fut le nom delle
2 CONT
2 CONT     En France deux fois royne
2 CONT
2 CONT     Duchesse des Bretons
2 CONT
2 CONT     Royale et Souveraine.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT It was made by an anonymous goldsmith of the court of Blois, perhaps drawn by Jean Perréal. In 1792, by order of the National Convention, the reliquary was exhumed, emptied, and seized as part of a collection of precious metals pe
2 CONC rtaining to churches, and sent to Nantes to be melted down. However, it was instead kept in the National Library, and was returned to Nantes in 1819, being kept in various museums, and in the Dobrée Museum since 1896.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne's will also conferred the succession of Brittany upon her second daughter, Renee. This was ignored by her husband, who confirmed Claude as Duchess and married her to Francis.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Personal characteristics
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne as Queen
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne was a highly intelligent woman who spent much of her time on the administration of Brittany. She was described as shrewd, proud and haughty in manner.[4] She made the safeguarding of Breton autonomy, and the preservation of t
2 CONC he Duchy outside the French crown, her life's work, although that goal would prove failed shortly after her death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne was also a patron of the arts and enjoyed music. A prolific collector of tapestries, it is very likely that the unicorn tapestries now on view at The Cloisters museum in New York City were commissioned by her in celebration o
2 CONC f her wedding to Louis XII.[5] She also commissioned a book of French manuscripts (a Book of Hours), known as The Great Hours of Anne of Brittany. She also instituted the Queen's Maids of Honour at the court.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of Anne's legs was shorter than the other, causing a limp. To fix the problem, she wore a higher heel on that leg.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne kept a box of gemstones. She would randomly pick one and give it to her visitors.[citation needed]
2 CONT
2 CONT She was a devoted mother, spending as much time as possible with her children. For her son, Charles-Orland, she commissioned a book of prayers, intended to be used in teaching him how to pray, and as a guidance to him as the futur
2 CONC e King of France; unfortunately, Charles-Orland died in 1495, and no other son lived more than a few weeks.
2 CONT
2 CONT At her marriage to Charles VIII, aged 14, Anne was described as a young and rosy-cheeked girl; by the time of her marriage to Louis, aged 22, after seven pregnancies with no surviving children, she was described as pale-faced and 
2 CONC wan. By the end of her life, at 36, she had been pregnant 14 times, with seven of the children stillborn. Of the remaining seven, only two survived childhood.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Marriages and Issue
2 CONT
2 CONT Medal of Anne of Brittany
2 CONT
2 CONT Tomb of the children at Tours Cathedral
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne's first marriage ceremony, on 19 December 1490, was a marriage by proxy to Maximilian of Habsburg. It was dissolved by the Pope in the following year; because it was only by proxy (rather than in person), it is not generally 
2 CONC considered a 'real' marriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her second husband was Charles VIII of France, whom she married at Chateau Langeais on 6 December 1491. She was pregnant by him seven times:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Charles Orlando, Dauphin of France. Her only healthy son, he lived 1492-1495, but died of the measles.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * A still-born son. She became pregnant in late 1492/early 1493, but travelled with her husband from castle to castle; she went into labour during a drive in the forest of Courcelles, and the child was premature and stillborn 
2 CONC (August 1493).
2 CONT
2 CONT     * A still-born girl. Anne became pregnant again five months after her miscarriage, and avoided travel (instead residing in Amboise near the Dauphin). However, in February 1494, she accompanied the King to Lyons, where he was p
2 CONC reparing to depart for the Italian Wars, and after arriving on 15 March, attended all the ceremonies; the stress of the occasion caused her to go into premature labour, and she gave birth to a still-born girl.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * A still-born child. She became pregnant again in August 1494, but lost the baby soon after.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Charles, Dauphin of France. He lived from 8 September to 2 October 1496. His death prompted Anne to withdraw to Moulins temporarily in despair.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Francis, Dauphin of France. He died several hours after his birth in 1497.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Anne of France. She died on the day of her birth, 20 March 1498 at Plessis les Tours.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her third husband was Louis XII of France. She was pregnant by him seven times:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Claude of France (1499 -1524) became her heir and also Queen Consort of Francis I.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Stillborn son (1500).
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Stillborn son (21 January 1503).
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Some sources cited a miscarriage by the end of 1503.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * In 1505 she suffered a miscarriage.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Stillborn son (1508) -some sources cited this was a miscarriage-.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Some sources cited a miscarriage in 1509.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Renée of France (1510 - 1575) married Ercole II d'Este, the Duke of Ferrara, and became the Duchess of Chartres.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Stillborn son (January 1512).
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Cultural symbolism of Anne
2 CONT
2 CONT Even while she was alive, the royal propaganda of Charles VIII and of Louis XII introduced Anne of Brittany as a perfect queen, a symbol of union and peace between the kingdom of France and the duchy of Brittany. In the following 
2 CONC centuries, historians and popular culture sometimes presented Anne of Brittany in differing fashions, ascribing to her physical and psychological characteristics that are not necessarily supported by historical evidence.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1991, the five-hundredth anniversary of the marriage of Anne of Brittany and Charles VIII of France was celebrated in Langeais. In Rennes, which had paid the price of this marriage by siege, food shortage, and an occupation, it
2 CONC  was hardly mentioned.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Depictions in fiction
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Anne of Brittany is the subject of Eleanor Fairburn's historical novel, Crowned Ermine, pub. 1968
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Anne in Breton culture
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne depicted by the Breton sculptor Jean Boucher in 1915
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne of Brittany is one of Brittany's most renowned historical figures, second perhaps only to Saint Yves. In testimony exist a large number of trades, hotels and street names bearing her name. Anne is also referred to by:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * The folksong "Anne de Bretagne: duchess en sabots" which imagines her wearing rustic clogs at her first meeting with the king.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * a song of Gilles Servat which evokes her life: Koc'h ki gwenn ha koc'h ki du
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Duchesse Anne, name of a beer produced in Brittany
2 CONT
2 CONT     * the square three-masted ship Duchesse Anne, currently moored in the Port of Dunkirk.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Anne de Bretagne, an opera composed by Louis-Albert Bourgault-Ducoudray in the 1870s. Bourgault, himself distantly related to Anne, was also born in Nantes and aimed to promote Breton culture throughout much of his music.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Anne de Bretagne, an opera by Breton composer Pierick Houdy, libretto by M. Fournereau, first performed in Rennes 2001, featuring Agnès Bove in the title role.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Anna Vreiz, a rock opera by Alan Simon, first performed in Nantes in June 2009 starring James Wood, Cécile Corbel, Fairport Convention, Nilda Fernandez, Tri Yann, Les Holroyd.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * If dead died, an anonymous poem going back to her funeral, and now performed by the popular Breton folk musicians Tri Yann. Another song of their repertory refers to the Duchess.
2 CONT
2 CONT There are several explanations for this: the destiny of this duchess who married three kings, including two kings of France, and who was only a child when she had to marry the first (even if early engagements were normal at that t
2 CONC ime); the historical role of Anne in the union of the duchy to the kingdom of France; the fact that very little of the history of Brittany is taught in Breton schools (the official school syllabus being written in Paris for all th
2 CONC e French territory -territories of overseas included - those retain only Anne as a notable Breton). This established fact leads some to experience the History of Brittany starting and finishing with Anne.
2 CONT
