1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Odet_de_Foix
2 CONT
2 CONT Odet de Foix, Vicomte de Lautrec (1485 – 15 August 1528) was a French military leader. He gained the reputation of a gallant and able soldier, but this scarcely seems to be justified by the facts, although he was always badly used
2 CONC  by fortune.
2 CONT
2 CONT The branch of the Viscounts of Lautrec originated with Pierre, the son of John III of Foix; Pierre's elder brother was Gaston IV of Foix.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Charlotte d'Albret (1495–1527) in 1520 and had several children:
2 CONT
2 CONT  Gaston (1522–28)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Henry (1523–40)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Claude (d.1549)
2 CONT
2 CONT  Francis (d.1528)
2 CONT
2 CONT Odet de Foix and his two brothers, the seigneur de Lescun and the seigneur de l'Esparre or Asparros, served Francis I of France as captains; and the influence of their sister, Françoise de Châteaubriant, who became the king's mist
2 CONC ress, gained them high office. In 1515 Lautrec took part in the campaign of Marignano.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1516 Lautrec received the government of the Milanese duchy, but by his severity made the French domination insupportable. In 1521 he succeeded in defending the duchy against the Spanish army, but in 1522 he was completely defea
2 CONC ted at the Battle of Bicocca, and was forced to evacuate the Milanese. The mutiny of his Swiss troops had compelled him, against his wish, to engage in the battle.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was created a marshal of France, and in 1527 he again received the command of the army of Italy. He occupied the Milanese, and was then sent to undertake the conquest of the kingdom of Naples. The defection of Andrea Doria and 
2 CONC an outbreak of the plague in the French camp brought on a fresh disaster. Lautrec himself caught the infection, and died in the August 1528.
