2 CONT
2 CONT Anna, Queen of Bohemia and Hungary, also sometimes known as Anna Jagellonica (July 23, 1503 – January 27, 1547) was, by marriage to Ferdinand I, King of the Romans and later Holy Roman Emperor, Queen of the Romans, and heiress of 
2 CONC the kingdoms of Hungary and Bohemia. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the elder child and only daughter of king Vladislaus II of Bohemia and Hungary (1456-1516) and his fourth wife Anna of Foix-Candale. She was an older sister of Louis II of Hungary and Bohemia, and his eventual heiress. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Her paternal grandparents were King Casimir IV of Poland, Grand Duke of Lithuania, of the Jagiellon dynasty, and Elisabeth of Austria, one of the heiresses of Bohemia, duchy of Luxembourg and duchy of Kujavia. Her maternal grandpa
2 CONC rents were Gaston de Foix, Count of Candale and Catherine de Foix, Infanta of the Kingdom of Navarre. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Life
2 CONT
2 CONT She was born in Prague and for the first three years of her life was the heiress presumptive to the thrones of Bohemia and the Kingdom of Hungary. The birth of her younger brother Louis on July 1, 1506, demoted her to second-in-li
2 CONC ne. Her mother died on July 26 of the same year. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The death of Vladislaus II on March 13, 1516 left both siblings in the care of Maximilian I, Holy Roman Emperor. It was arranged that Anna marry his grandson Archduke Ferdinand of Austria, second son of Queen regnant Joanna of Cas
2 CONC tile and her late husband and co-ruler Philip I of Castile. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna married Ferdinand on May 25, 1521 in Linz, Austria. At the time Ferdinand was governing the Habsburg hereditary lands on behalf of his older brother Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Her brother Louis was killed in the Battle of Mohács against Suleiman the Magnificent of the Ottoman Empire on August 29, 1526. This left the thrones of both Bohemia and Hungary vacant, and Anna being the closest living relative o
2 CONC f Louis, Ferdinand claimed both kingdoms in her right and was elected King of Bohemia on October 24 of the same year. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hungary was a more difficult case. Suleiman had annexed much of its lands. Ferdinand was proclaimed King of Hungary by a group of nobles, but another faction of Hungarian nobles refused to allow a foreign ruler to hold that title 
2 CONC and elected John Zápolya as an alternative king. The resulting conflict between the two rivals and their successors lasted until 1571. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1531 Ferdinand's older brother Charles V recognised Ferdinand as his successor as Holy Roman Emperor, and Ferdinand was elevated to the title King of the Romans. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna and Ferdinand had fifteen children, which was a boon to Bohemia and Hungary, both of which kingdoms had suffered for centuries from premature deaths among heirs and from a shortage of succession prospects. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile Anna served as queen consort of Bohemia and as one of two rival queen consorts of Hungary until her death. She died in her native Prague. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1556 Charles V abdicated and Ferdinand succeeded as emperor, nine years after Anna's death. 
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit]Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna and Ferdinand had fifteen children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth of Austria (July 9, 1526 - June 15, 1545). In 1543 she was married to king Sigismund II Augustus of Poland and Lithuania, Anna's paternal first cousin, but did not have children. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Maximilian (July 31, 1527 - October 12, 1576), the future emperor. Each of Maximilian's sons died without surviving issue, and the only child of his who left heirs was Anna, named after grandmother, whose son was Philip III of Spa
2 CONC in. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna of Austria (July 7, 1528 - October 16/October 17, 1590). Married Albert V, Duke of Bavaria. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Archduke Ferdinand II of Austria (June 14, 1529 - January 24, 1595), the future Archduke of Further Austria. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Maria of Austria (May 15, 1531–December 11, 1581). Married William, Duke of Jülich-Cleves-Berg. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Magdalena of Austria (August 14, 1532 - September 10, 1590). A nun. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Catharina of Austria (September 15, 1533 - February 28, 1572). In 1553 she was married to king Sigismund II Augustus of Poland and Lithuania, Anna's paternal first cousin and her own sister's (Elisabeth's) widower, but did not hav
2 CONC e children. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleanor of Austria (November 2, 1534 - August 5, 1594). Married William I, Duke of Mantua. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Margaret of Austria (February 16, 1536 - March 12, 1567). A nun. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Archduke John of Austria (April 10, 1538 - March 20, 1539). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Barbara of Austria (April 30, 1539 - September 19, 1572). Married Alfonso II, Duke of Ferrara, but did not have children. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Archduke Charles II of Austria (June 3, 1540 - July 10, 1590), the future Archduke of Inner Austria, who was to become father of Ferdinand II, Holy Roman Emperor. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ursula of Austria (July 24, 1541 - April 30, 1543). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Helena of Austria (January 7, 1543 - March 5, 1574). A nun. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Johanna of Austria (January 24, 1547 - April 10, 1578). Married Francis I, Grand Duke of Tuscany.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anna_of_Bohemia_and_Hungary
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Ann, Sister of King of Hungary
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://www.geni.com/profile/index/6000000003951118666?fsession_id=1243065486611&from_flash=1&through=6000000003950912492
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna, Queen of Bohemia and Hungary, also sometimes known as Anna Jagellonica (Buda (now Budapest), Hungary, July 23, 1503 – Prague, Bohemia, January 27, 1547) was, by marriage to Ferdinand I, King of the Romans and later Holy Roma
2 CONC n Emperor, Queen of the Romans, and heiress presumptive of the kingdoms of Hungary and Bohemia.
2 CONT
2 CONT She was the elder child and only daughter of king Vladislaus II of Bohemia and Hungary (1456-1516) and his third wife Anna of Foix-Candale. She was an older sister of Louis II of Hungary and Bohemia, and his eventual heiress.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her paternal grandparents were King Casimir IV of Poland, Grand Duke of Lithuania, of the Jagiellon dynasty, and Elisabeth of Austria, one of the heiresses of Bohemia, duchy of Luxembourg and duchy of Kujavia. Her maternal grandpa
2 CONC rents were Gaston de Foix, Count of Candale and Catherine de Foix, Infanta of the Kingdom of Navarre
2 CONT
2 CONT She was born in Buda (now Budapest) and for the first three years of her life was the heiress presumptive to the thrones of Bohemia and the Kingdom of Hungary. The birth of her younger brother Louis on July 1, 1506, demoted her to
2 CONC  second-in-line. Her mother died on July 26 of the same year.
2 CONT
2 CONT The death of Vladislaus II on March 13, 1516 left both siblings in the care of Maximilian I, Holy Roman Emperor. It was arranged that Anna marry his grandson Archduke Ferdinand of Austria, second son of Queen regnant Joanna of Cas
2 CONC tile and her late husband and co-ruler Philip I of Castile.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna married Ferdinand on May 25, 1521 in Linz, Austria. At the time Ferdinand was governing the Habsburg hereditary lands on behalf of his older brother Charles V, Holy Roman Emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her brother Louis was killed in the Battle of Mohács against Suleiman the Magnificent of the Ottoman Empire on August 29, 1526. This left the thrones of both Bohemia and Hungary vacant, and Anna being the closest living relative o
2 CONC f Louis, Ferdinand claimed both kingdoms in her right and was elected King of Bohemia on October 24 of the same year.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hungary was a more difficult case. Suleiman had annexed much of its lands. Ferdinand was proclaimed King of Hungary by a group of nobles, but another faction of Hungarian nobles refused to allow a foreign ruler to hold that title 
2 CONC and elected John Zápolya as an alternative king. The resulting conflict between the two rivals and their successors lasted until 1571.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1531 Ferdinand's older brother Charles V recognised Ferdinand as his successor as Holy Roman Emperor, and Ferdinand was elevated to the title King of the Romans.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna and Ferdinand had fifteen children, which was a boon to Bohemia and Hungary, both of which kingdoms had suffered for centuries from premature deaths among heirs and from a shortage of succession prospects.
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile Anna served as queen consort of Bohemia and as one of three existing queen consorts of Hungary until her death. She died in Prague.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1556 Charles V abdicated and Ferdinand succeeded as emperor, nine years after Anna's death.
