2 CONT ===Magna Carta Sureties===
2 CONT '''Page 77'''
2 CONT '''Line 59A'''
2 CONT (Prepared by Douglas Richardson)
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. WILLIAM DE FORTIBUS (also styled le Fort), son and heir, d. sh. Bef. 22 May 1259, lord of Chewton, Somerset, m. c. 30 July 1247 '''MAUD (or MATILDA) DE FERRERS (149-3), b. c. 1230, d. 12 Mar 1298/9, widow of Simon IV de Kyme, d
2 CONC . 1248, lord of Sotby, co. Lincoln''', and dau. of William Ferrers, Earl of Derby by his wife, Sibyl Marshal… (''CP'' IV, 199 chart; Sanders, ''Baronies'', pp. 9, 38-39, 62-64, 79-80. Knights of Edward I, II 80, IV 156; ''Cal. Inq
2 CONC .p.m''. 1: 298, 3: 400-401; ''Cal. Patent Roll''s, Henry III, 1247-1258, p. 23; ''List of Ancient Correspondence of the Chancery and Exchequer, Lists & Indexes'', no. XV, p. 355)   [https://www.google.com/books/edition/The_Magna_C
2 CONC harta_Sureties_1215/59XcwoRK9jkC?hl=en&gbpv=1&dq=Simon+de+Kyme+Lord+of+Sotby&pg=PA77&printsec=frontcover]
2 CONT ----
2 CONT MAUDE (or MATILDA) DE FERRERS, Vicomtesse de Rochechouart, born about 1230, died 12 March 1298/9 
2 CONT ===Family===
2 CONT Maud is one of the 7 daughters (coheirs to their mother) of William (Ferrers), Earl of Derby. [Complete Peerage] [7] [8]
2 CONT
2 CONT *married (1) Simon de Kyme of Kyme, died shortly before 20 July 1248, s.p., [2] 
2 CONT *married (2) about 30 July 1248 William de Fortibus (also styled le Fort) died shortly before 22 May 1259, [3] lord of Chewton, Somerset, son of Hugh de Viviona, Sheriff of Somerset and Dorset, by his wife Mabel, daughter and cohe
2 CONC ir of WILLIAM MALET (234A-238), Magna Carta surety, 1215 [4];
2 CONT * married (3) by 4 Feb. 1267, Sir Emery (or Aimery), Vicomte de Rochechouart in Poitou, living 1284." [5] [6]
2 CONT
2 CONT William de Vivonne and Maud de Ferrers had four daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Joan de Vivonne,[3] married first to Sir Ingram de Percy, second to Aimery X de Rochechouart, and third to Sir Reynold Fitz Peter[1]
2 CONT # Sibyl de Vivonne,[3] born about 1253, married Guy de Rochechouart[1]
2 CONT # Mabel de Vivonne,[3] born about 1255, died before 1 November 1299, married Fulk (or Foucaud) D'Archiac[1]
2 CONT # Cecily de Vivonne,[3] born in 1257, died 10 January 1320/1, married Sir John de Beauchamp and had four children[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography===
2 CONT http://www.histoirepassion.eu/?Three-generations-of-the-Anglo-Poitou-family-of-Vivonne-from-13th-century
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Matilda (or Maud) de Ferrers (c.1230-1299)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT William de Fortibus (?-1259)‘s  wife Matilda de Ferrers was related to many of the most powerful Anglo-Norman families in England. Of special note was her maternal grandfather, the same William Marshal who had ordered Hugh to hand
2 CONC  over Bristol Castle twenty years earlier. Earl of Pembroke by right of his wife Isabel de Clare, William had begun life as a penniless and landless fourth son. He became a “knight errant” and made his own way in the world by seiz
2 CONC ing valuable hostages at melees. Thereafter he gave unflinching loyalty to the king even when, at one point, John confiscated all his estates. Aged about 70, he fought in full armour on horseback at the 1216 battle of Lincoln and 
2 CONC until his death in 1219 he had been one of the three regents of the minority government of the boy king Henry III. [15]
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the Marshal’s ten children was Matilda’s mother, Sybil Marshal who married William de Ferrers earl of Derby. They had seven daughters and when Sybil died, probably about 1238, the earl remarried and finally had a male heir,
2 CONC  Robert. [16]
2 CONT
2 CONT So Matilda, born probably in about 1230, one of seven daughters of a mother who was one of ten children, five of them sons, did not start out with a particularly promising personal inheritance in prospect. By 1245, however, that h
2 CONC ad changed dramatically and Matilda had become an heiress in her own right. In that year Anselm, the last of her five Marshal uncles, was dead and none of them left any legitimate children as heirs. Consequently, in 1245, the Pemb
2 CONC roke estates were broken up to be divided between the Marshal sisters or their descendants like Matilda.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some time in May 1248 when she was about 18, Matilda’s first, childless marriage ended with the death of her husband, Simon de Kyme. Two months later the king had granted her remarriage to William de Fortibus, and she swore her oa
2 CONC th of allegiance. [17]
2 CONT
2 CONT Eleven years later William had died and Matilda did not remarry for five years until in 1264 she contracted her third marriage to the recently widowed Aimery (IX) viscount de Rochechouart. [18] There is no evidence that Matilda an
2 CONC d Aimery had any children together but this time her marriage lasted over twenty years until Aimery died in about 1286. The Calendar of Patent Rolls has several entries indicating that the couple travelled to and from England seve
2 CONC ral times, the first in 1269 [19] naming lawyers presumably to deal with legal disputes and transactions concerning the Vivonne and Pembroke family estates in England
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT The name of Joan de Vivonne's father as reported by Complete Peerage is slightly askew. His name was actually Sir William de Forz, not William le Fort de Vivonne. He died shortly before 22 May 1259. Sir William's wife and mother o
2 CONC f his children was Maud de Ferrers, widow of Simon de Kyme, and daughter of William de Ferrers, Knt., 5th Earl of Derby. Sir William de Forz in turn was the son and heir of Sir Hugh (or Hugues) de Vivonne, of Chewton, Somerset, by
2 CONC  Mabel, the daughter and heiress of William Malet, the Magna Carta baron.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===William de Ferrers, 5th Earl of Derby===
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family and children'''
2 CONT
2 CONT William Ferrers married Sibyl Marshal, one of the daughters and co-heirs of William Marshal, 1st Earl of Pembroke. They had seven daughters:
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes Ferrers (died May 11, 1290), married William de Vesci.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabel Ferrers (died before November 26, 1260), married (1) Gilbert Basset, of Wycombe, and (2) Reginald de Mohun
2 CONT
2 CONT Maud Ferrers (died March 12, 1298), married (1) Simon de Kyme, and (2) William de Vivonia, and (3) Amaury IX of Rochechouart.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Maud's married name was Maud de Kyme.
2 CONT
2 CONT Maud, vicomtesse de Rochechouart, first married Simon de Kyme before 1248. She next married our ancestor William de Fortibus, Lord of Chewton, son of Hugh de Vivonia, Sheriff of Somerset and Dorset and Mabel Malet, circa 30 July 1
2 CONC 248. She married Vicomte de Rochechouart Emery de Rochechouart before 4 February 1267 in Poitou, France.
2 CONT
2 CONT See "My Lines"
2 CONT ( http://homepages.rootsweb.ancestry.com/~cousin/html/p407.htm#i8270 )
2 CONT
2 CONT from Compiler: R. B. Stewart, Evans, GA
2 CONT ( http://homepages.rootsweb.ancestry.com/~cousin/html/index.htm ).
2 CONT
