0 @N6000000002909004294@ NOTE Isabella of Angoulême (Fr. Isabelle d'Angoulême ; (1188[1] – 31 May 1246) was Countess of Angoulême and queen consort of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Queen of England
1 CONT She was the only daughter and heir of Aymer Taillefer, Count of Angoulême, by Alix de Courtenay. Her paternal grandparents were William V Taillefer, Count of Angouleme and Marguerite de Turenne. Her maternal grandparents were Pier
1 CONC re de Courtenay and Elizabeth de Courtenay. Her maternal great-grandfather was King Louis VI of France. She became Countess of Angoulême in her own right in 1202, by which time she was already queen of England. Her marriage to Kin
1 CONC g John took place on 24 August 1200, at Bordeaux, a year after he annulled his first marriage to Isabel of Gloucester. At the time of this marriage Isabella was aged about twelve, and her beauty was renowned; she is sometimes call
1 CONC ed the "Helen" of the Middle Ages by historians.
1 CONT
1 CONT It could not be said to have been a successful marriage, as Isabella was much younger than her husband and had a fiery character to match his. Before their marriage, she had been betrothed to Hugh le Brun, Count of Lusignan[2], so
1 CONC n of the then Count of La Marche. As a result of John's temerity in taking her as his second wife, King Philip II of France confiscated all his French lands, and armed conflict ensued.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Second marriage
1 CONT When John died in 1216, Isabella was still in her twenties. She returned to France and in 1220 proceeded to marry Hugh X of Lusignan, now Count of La Marche, her former fiancé. By him, Isabella had nine more children. Their eldest
1 CONC  son Hugh XI of Lusignan succeeded his father as Count of La Marche and Count of Angouleme in 1249.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Death and burial
1 CONT Isabella was accused of plotting against King Louis IX of France in 1244; she fled to Fontevrault Abbey, where she died on 31 May 1246, and was buried there. At her own insistence she was first buried in the churchyard, as an act 
1 CONC of repentance for her many misdeeds. On a visit to Fontevrault her son King Henry III of England was shocked to find her buried outside the Abbey and ordered her immediately moved inside. She was finally placed beside Henry II and
1 CONC  Eleanor of Aquitaine. Afterwards most of her many children, having few prospects in France, set sail for England and the court of their half-brother King Henry III.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Issue
1 CONT With King John of England: 5 children, all of whom survived into adulthood, including: 
1 CONT King Henry III of England (1 October 1207 – 16 November 1272) Married Eleanor of Provence 
1 CONT Richard, Earl of Cornwall and King of the Romans (5 January 1209 – 2 April 1272). Married firstly Isabel Marshal, secondly Sanchia of Provence, and thirdly Beatrice of Falkenburg. 
1 CONT Joan (22 July 1210 – 1238), the wife of King Alexander II of Scotland 
1 CONT Isabella (1214–1241), the wife of Emperor Frederick II 
1 CONT Eleanor (1215–1275), who would marry firstly William Marshal, 2nd Earl of Pembroke, and secondly Simon de Montfort, 6th Earl of Leicester. 
1 CONT With Hugh X of Lusignan, the Count of La Marche: nine children, all of whom survived into adulthood, including: 
1 CONT Hugh XI of Lusignan (1221–1250), Count of La Marche and Count of Angoulême. Married Yolande de Dreux, Countess of Penthièvre and of Porhoet 
1 CONT Aymer de Valence (1222–1260), Bishop of Winchester 
1 CONT Agnès de Lusignan (1223–1269), married William II de Chauvigny 
1 CONT Alice le Brun de Lusignan (1224 – 9 February 1256), married John de Warenne, 7th Earl of Surrey and had issue 
1 CONT Guy de Lusignan (c. 1225 – 1264), killed at the Battle of Lewes. (Tufton Beamish maintains that he escaped to France after the Battle of Lewes and died there in 1269) 
1 CONT Geoffrey de Lusignan (c. 1226 – 1274), married in 1259 Jeanne, Viscountess of Châtellerault and had issue 
1 CONT William de Valence, 1st Earl of Pembroke (c. 1228 – 1296) Married Joan de Munchensi. Had issue 
1 CONT Marguerite de Lusignan (c. 1229 – 1288), married 1243 Raymond VII of Toulouse, married c. 1246 Aimery IX de Thouars, Viscount of Thouars 
1 CONT Isabelle de Lusignan (1234 – 14 January 1299), married Geoffrey de Ranc
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT ^ The Complete Peerage 
1 CONT ^ Hugues X of Lusignan 
1 CONT
