0 @N6000000003949767061@ NOTE William de Valence, styled but never invested as Earl of Pembroke. [Burke's Peerage]
1 CONT Lord Valence, Crusader 6 Mar 1250.   Earldom of Pemborke: Nevertheless Henry III's half-brother William de Valence, who married a niece of these last five [all sons of William Marshal, who died dspl] Earls of Pembroke, is sometime
1 CONC s referred to by contemporary sources as Earl of Pembroke. His Aspirations in that direction were promoted by the fact that Henry III had presented him with Pembroke Castle. In addition de Valence's widow Joan by dint of usage, pl
1 CONC us inheritance of the feudal Lordship of Pembroke, came to be known as Countess of Pembroke. And their son, Aymer de Valence, who was no more formally created Earl of Pembroke than his father had been, was nevertheless coming to b
1 CONC e generally recognized as Earl of Pembroke by some ten years after his father's death. Aymer's wife founded Pembroke College Cambridge. [Burke's Peerage, p. 2217]
1 CONT In 1247 came to Eng; HENRY  III granted him many properties including Goodrich; Pembroke; Hereford and Hertford Castles and app him to many important    posts; On 2 Oct 1249 app joint amb. to Fr; In 1249/50 to Holy    Lands; In Oc
1 CONC t 1253-Sep 1254 with king in Aquitaine; In Sept 1255 with king in the North; In 1257 argued with SIMON de    MONTFORT and in 1258 he, his 2 brs and b-in-law Er SURREY fell out with barons and took refuse in Wolvesey Castle,    Win
1 CONC chester, then capture but allowed to leave for Poitou;    About Easter 1261 returned and restored; In 1264 supported King at siege of Northampton then battle of Lewes (14 May    1264) after which defeat he fled to Fr; In June 1265
1 CONC  at Siege of Gloucester, attack on Kenilworth and at battle of Eversham    (1 Aug 1265); In 1265 granted Haverford Castle, which he had    captured from HUMPHREY de BOHUN, for the term of the minority of the de BOHUN heir; In 1266
1 CONC , with Er SURREY attacked Bury    St. Edmunds; In Aug 1270 left for Palestine with pr Ed, by
1 CONT     Jan 1272/3 back in London; 1273-79 often in Aquitaine; In 1277-1294 served against Welsh; granted Abergaveny during    minority of his s-in-law JOHN de HASTINGS; In 1295 sum to parl. as Er PEMBROKE but title never formally inv
1 CONC ested; m 13    Aug 1247 Joan (1222 Wales-bfr 20 Sep 1307) d&ch of WARINE de  MONCHENSY (-c1254) Lord of SWANSCOMBE, Kent by his m1 (to whom Joan was heir following death of her br in June 1247)    Joan 5d&ch of WILLIAM MARSHAL 4Er
1 CONC  PEMBROKE; In 1255 the
1 CONT     guardianship of Joan's half-br WILLIAM de MONCHENCY, (who had an only d Dionysia) was given to WILLIAM This William d 1288  on attack on REES ap GRIFFITH at Drosselan Castle.  Other refs show WILLIAM as full br of Joan Monchen
1 CONC sey
