0 @N6000000000269706015@ NOTE Please find below a revised listing of the bastard children of King John.  The list has been augmented by many new references, corrections, and additions since my last posting of this listing.  The new listing includes Philip "Fiz
1 CONC  Le Rey" [i.e., Philip the king's son]) found in a 1263 Sussex fine as a possible bastard child of King John.
1 CONT
1 CONT Also, it appears there may be a link between Isabel, wife of Richard Fitz Ives, and another royal bastard, Eudes Fitz Roy.  I've learned that in 1245, Isabel's son and heir, William Fitz Ives, sued Giles de Chanceaux regarding pro
1 CONC perty in Cornwall.  This is surely the same man who was granted Eudes Fitz Roy's properties in Essex on his death without issue in 1242.  The Fitz Ives and Chanceaux link deserves further study.
1 CONT
1 CONT Comments are invited.
1 CONT
1 CONT Best always, Douglas Richardson, Salt Lake City, Utah
1 CONT
1 CONT - - - - - - - - - -
1 CONT
1 CONT REVISED LISTING OF BASTARD CHILDREN OF KING JOHN OF ENGLAND:
1 CONT
1 CONT Legitimated child of John of England, by a mistress, Clemence _____:
1 CONT
1 CONT i. JOAN OF ENGLAND, married LLYWELYN AP IORWERTH, Prince of North Wales [see WAKE 4].
1 CONT
1 CONT Illegitimate child of John of England, by a mistress, _____ de Warenne, daughter of Hamelin, 5th Earl of Surrey, by Isabel, daughter and heiress of William de Warenne, 3rd Earl of Surrey [see WARENNE 2]:
1 CONT
1 CONT i. RICHARD FITZ ROY (or DE WARENNE), Knt., Baron of Chilham, Kent, married ROSE DE DOVER [see ATHOLL 4].
1 CONT
1 CONT Illegitimate child of John of England, by a mistress, Hawise _____:
1 CONT
1 CONT i. OLIVER FITZ ROY.  He defended Wolvesey Castle for Peter des Roches, Bishop of Winchester, in 1216.  In 1217 he was granted the lands formerly held by Peter Fitz Herbert to sustain him in the king's service.  In 1218 he arrived 
1 CONC with other English knights at Damietta in the company of Papal legate, Pelayo.  He did not return.  H.R. Luard, Chronica Majora 3 (1876): 40-41.  C.P.R., 1216-1225 (1901), pp. 42-43,92.  S. Painter, Reign of King John (1949), pp. 
1 CONC 232-233.  NEHGR 119 (1965): 94-102.  J.M. Powell, Anatomy of a Crusade (1986), pg. 235.  N. Vincent, Peter des Roches (1996), pp. 71,175.
1 CONT
1 CONT Illegitimate children of John of England, by an unknown mistress (or mistresses):
1 CONT
1 CONT i. JOHN FITZ ROY, evidently a clerk, supported by the custodians of the see of Lincoln in 1201.  D.M. Stenton, Great Roll of the Pipe Michaelmas 1201 (Pipe Roll Soc., n.s., vol. 14) (1936), pp. xix,192-193.  S. Painter, Reign of K
1 CONC ing John (1949), pp. 232-233. NEHGR 119 (1965): 94-102.
1 CONT
1 CONT ii. GEOFFREY FITZ ROY.  In 1204 he received a loan by the pledge of William Longespée, Earl of Salisbury, and Peter de Stokes.  In 1205 he led an expedition into Poitou and died the same year.  Curia Regis Rolls 3 (1926): 321 (sui
1 CONC t dated 1205: " ... quam diu predictus Radulfus [de Trublevill] fuerit in servicio nostro in Pictavia cum Gaufrido filio nostro").  D.M. Stenton, Great Roll of the Pipe Michaelmas 1204 (Pipe Roll Soc., n.s., vol. 18) (1940), pp. x
1 CONC xxv-xxxvj,xlj,33,60,87,131.  D.M. Stenton, Great Roll of the Pipe Michaelmas 1205 (Pipe Roll Soc., n.s., vol. 19) (1941), pp. xviij-xix,19,79-80.  S. Painter, Reign of King John (1949), pp. 232-233.  NEHGR 119 (1965): 94-102.
1 CONT
1 CONT iii. HENRY FITZ ROY, Knt., of Waltham, Ashby, Brigsley, Gonerby (in Hatcliffe), Hawerby, and North Coates, co. Lincoln, and Chilham, Kent.  He was sent as a student to the Prior of Kenilworth in 1207.  In 1215 he was granted the l
1 CONC ands of Robert Fitz Walter in Cornwall.  In 1217 he and Ralph de Raleigh were granted the manor of Waltham, co. Lincoln formerly held by Alan Fitz Count to sustain them in royal service.  In 1231 he was granted all of the land of 
1 CONC Henry de Avaugor, a Norman, in Waltham, co. Lincoln.  He married before 1236 EVE DE BLANCHMINSTER (or WHITCHURCH), widow of William de Champernoun (living 1230), of Umberleigh (in Atherington) and High Bickington, Devon, and daugh
1 CONC ter and heiress of Reynold de Blanchminster (or Whitchurch) (living 1248), of Shrivenham and Winterbourne (in Chieveley), co. Berks, and Bolney, co. Oxford, by his 1st wife, Alice, daughter and co-heiress of Nicholas de Bolney.  T
1 CONC hey had no issue.  SIR HENRY FITZ ROY died shortly before 8 Apr. 1245.  His widow, Eve, married (3rd) before 30 June 1252 Giles de Clifford (living 1276).  C.Ch.R. 1 (1895): 137 (styled "Henry the king's brother").  J.L. Vivian, V
1 CONC isitations of the County of Devon (1895), pp. 160.  C.P.R., 1216-1225 (1901), pp. 128, 574 ("Henricus filius regis").  C.C.R., 1227-1231 (1902), pg. 51. C.P.R., 1225-1232 (1903), pp. 311 ("Henricus frater regis"), 357,441. C.C.R.,
1 CONC  1234-1237 (1908), pg. 219 ("Henrico fratri regis").  Book of Fees 1 (1920): 362 ("Henricus frater regis"), 617 ("Henricus filius regis"); 2 (1923): 660,675,1021 (instances of "Henricus filius Regis").  C.P.R., 1232-1247 (1906), p
1 CONC p. 20,296,450.  C.C.R., 1237-1242 (1911), pg. 511 ("Henricum filium regis").  VCH Berkshire 3 (19??): 424; 4 (1924): 63,513,533.  C.C.R., 1251-1253 (1927), pg. 116.  S. Painter, Reign of King John (1949), pp. 232-233.  C.P. 12 Pt.
1 CONC  2 (1959): 645 (sub Wilington).  Curia Regis Rolls 13 (1959): 138,215,283 ("Henricus filius regis John"), 290,364-365,371,514,542; 15 (1972): 83,449 (styled "Henricum filium le Rey").  R.A. Brown, Memoranda Roll for the Tenth Year
1 CONC  of the Reign of King John (1207-8) (Pipe Roll Soc., n.s., vol. 31) (1957), pg. 137 (Henry called "our son" [filio nostro] by King John).  NEHGR 119 (1965): 94-102.  B.R. Kemp, Reading Abbey Cartularies 1 (Camden, 4th ser., vol. 3
1 CONC 1) (1986): 375.  S.D. Church, Household Knights of King John (1999), pg. 127.
1 CONT
1 CONT iv. OSBERT GIFFORD, Knt. In 1215 he received the lands of Thomas de Ardern in Oxfordshire.  In 1216 he likewise received Ardern's lands in Bundes, Norfolk, and elsewhere in Suffolk, Essex, and Sussex, and the lands of Aumary Despe
1 CONC nser, Roger Fitz Nicholas, and Ralph Bluet in Oxfordshire.  SIR OSBERT GIFFORD died in 1248.  Ancestor 3 (1902): 227 (his arms: Ermine two bars gules, on a chief gules a leopard or). Genealogist n.s. 28 (1922): 128-129.  S. Painte
1 CONC r, Reign of King John (1949), pp. 232-233.  NEHGR 119 (1965): 94-102.  T.D. Tremlett, Rolls of Arms Henry III (Harleian Soc. Pub., vols. 113-114) (1967), pg. 47.
1 CONT
1 CONT v. EUDES (or IVES) FITZ ROY, of Canewdon, Essex.  In 1233 he was granted lands in Aldbury, co. Hertford.  He had an exchequer fee of £20 granted to him in 1237.  In the period, c. 1227/41, he witnessed a charter for his brother, R
1 CONC ichard, Earl of Cornwall, to Launceston Priory in Cornwall.  In 1240 he joined Earl Richard on a crusade to the Holy Land.  EUDES FITZ ROY died there testate shortly before 21 Jan. 1241/2.  His lands in Essex were granted to Giles
1 CONC  de Chanceaux in 1242.  No known descendants.  G. Oliver, Monasticon Diocesis Exonienses (1846), pg. 23.  C.C.R., 1231-1234 (1905), pg. 210 ("Eudoni filio regis").  C.P.R., 1232-1247 (1906), pp. 179 ("Eudo the king's brother"), 27
1 CONC 0 ("Eudo son of R. the king's brother"), 314 ("Eudo the king's brother").  C.C.R., 1237-1242 (1911), pp. 73 ("Eudoni fratri regis"), 386-387 ("Eudonis fratris regis"), 387 ("Ivonis fratris domini regis"), 532 ("Eudonis fratris nos
1 CONC tri").  Cal. Liberate Rolls 1 (1916), pg. 263.  N. Denholm-Young, Richard of Cornwall (1947), pp. 41,112.  P.L. Hull, Cartulary of Launceston Priory (Devon & Cornwall Rec. Soc., n.s., vol. 30) (1987) 12 ("Yvo brother of the earl" 
1 CONC [Richard, Earl of Cornwall]).
1 CONT
1 CONT vi. BARTHOLOMEW FITZ ROY, clerk, papal chaplain, member of the order of Friars Preachers, living Aug. 1254.  Papal Registers: Letters 1 (1893): 281,286,305.
1 CONT
1 CONT vii. MAUD FITZ ROY, nun, elected Abbess of Barking 5 Aug. 1247; died shortly before 6 Feb. 1252.  Sir William Dugdale, Monasticon Anglicanum 1 (1817): 437,441 ("Dame Maud la file le Roy John").  VCH Essex 2 (1907): 120.
1 CONT
1 CONT Alleged illegitimate child of John of England, by an unknown mistress, _____:
1 CONT
1 CONT i. ISABEL FITZ ROY, married RICHARD FITZ IVES, Knt., lord of Degembris (in Newlyn East), Cornwall.  They had two sons, William Fitz Richard, Knt. (of Degembris and Penhallow [both in Newlyn East], Lanisley (in Gulival), and Rosney
1 CONC thon and Trenoweth-Chammon [both in St. Keverne]) and Richard Fitz Richard (clerk), and one daughter, Isabel (wife of Belyn Hellegan, Knt.).  He gave tithes in Gruguth (in St. Keverne), Cornwall.  SIR RICHARD FITZ IVES allegedly d
1 CONC ied in 1207.  Modern descendants (not traced).  Herald and Genealogist 7 (1873): 229-231 (Isabel styled "filie Regis Joh'is").  Sir John Maclean, Parochial and Family Hist. of the Deanery of Trigg Minor 1 (1876): 317.  J.L. Vivian
1 CONC , Visitations of Cornwall (1887), pg. 30.  G.D. Stawell, A Quantock Family: The Stawells of Cothelstone and their Descendants (1910), pp. 44-45.  J.H. Rowe, Cornwall Feet of Fines 1 (1914): 17,49,54-55,58-59,91-92,171,438-440.  C.
1 CONC R.R. 11 (1955), pg. 473; 12 (1957): 189.  NEHGR 119 (1965): 94-102.  C.R.R. 18 (1999): 362-363. Harleian MS. 4031, ff. 76b,81.
1 CONT
1 CONT Possible illegitimate child of John of England, by an unknown mistress, _____:
1 CONT
1 CONT i. PHILIP FITZ ROY (possible child).  In 1263 he and his wife, Lavina, conveyed lands at Bignor, Pebmarsh, and Petworth, Surrey to Henry Fitz [le] Roy, perhaps their son.  L.F. Salzman, Abstract of Feet of Fines Relating to the Co
1 CONC unty of Sussex (Sussex Rec. Soc., vol. 7) (1908), pg. 45 ("Ph[illip]um Fiz Le Rey" [i.e., Philip the king's son]) (cf. W. Hudson, Three Earliest Subsidies for the County of Sussex in the Years 1296,1327,1332 (Sussex Rec. Soc., vol
1 CONC . 19) (1910), pp. 73,95 (references to "Henr[icus] fyz le Rey" on 1296 Sussex subsidy).
1 CONT
1 CONT The following is a list of eleven bastard children of King John of England which I've assembled from a variety of sources.  If anyone has any additions or corrections to this material, please let me know. The first two children li
1 CONC sted, Richard Fitz Roy (or de Warenne), Baron of Chilham, Kent, and Joan, wife of Llywelyn ap Iowerth, Prince of North Wales, have many modern descendants.
1 CONT
1 CONT Although sometimes questioned, I accept Isabel, wife of Richard Fitz Ives, Knt., as a bastard daughter of King John.  Isabel appears to have modern descendants.  Besides Isabel, I've also included a new bastard child for King John
1 CONC  not commonly found on such lists, namely Maud Fitz Roy, Abbess of Barking, whose identity is proven by the sources cited below.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sidney Painter, King John's historian, says of him: ".... cruel, lecherous, and deceitful."  Surely the number of illegitimate children listed below for King John proves the lecherous part.
1 CONT
1 CONT Best always, Douglas Richardson, Salt Lake City, Utah
1 CONT
1 CONT E-mail: royalancestry@msn.com
