1 CONC  dramatic result of Johns actions was the collaps e of the projected French invasion, leaving the French King Phillippe furious.  John ordered his fleet into the Swine estuary where Phillipp e had ordered his fleet.  Johns fleet u
1 CONC nder the command of William of Salisbury found Phillippes fleet relatively unguarded and inflicted se vere damage but was ultimately driven off.  Johns fleet returned to En gland with a minor victory with its decks loaded none the
1 CONC  less with gr eat prizes.  On St. Margarets day of 1213, John was absolved of his ex communication by Archbishop Stephen Langdon of Canterbury.  John, eage r to hit Phillippe while he was weak, ordered an expedition to Poitou , bu
1 CONC t met with argument from his barons especially those in the north .  Towards the end of 1213, John called a great council at Oxford to d iscuss preparations for another invasion of France.  He called barons , knights and 'four pru
1 CONC dent' men from each area.  He became the first English king to include ordinary freeman of the shires to a council o f the realm.  John's allies at this time included Otto of Germany, th e Counts of Holland, Boulogne and Flanders 
1 CONC as well as the Dukes of Lor raine and Brabant.  In the south of France, he was allied with Peter I I of Aragon and Raymond VI of Toulouse.  The plan to attack France wa s that the coalition forces from the low contries would strik
