1 CONT Name Suffix:<NSFX> King of England
0 @N6000000003495355416@ NOTE The following is from the Grolier Multimedia Encyclopedia Online. Grolier Interactive Inc. <http://gi.grolier.com/encyclopedial>. January 2, 1998:
1 CONT
1 CONT The youngest son of Henry II and Eleanor of Aquitaine, John, b. Dec. 24, 1167, succeeded his brother Richard I as king of England on May 27, 1199. John's reign is notable for his difficulties with the church and the barons; the ki
1 CONC ng's conflict with the latter resulted in the Magna Carta. 
1 CONT John's character was not attractive--he was hedonistic, mercurial, personally unstable, suspicious, and unforgiving. Yet he had many commendable qualities--he was highly intelligent, well versed in law and government, efficient, a
1 CONC nd sophisticated. His greatest shortcoming, in view of his contemporaries, was that he was no warrior, in an age when kings were expected to be great fighters. Moreover, John's difficulties stemmed largely from the policies of his
1 CONC  father and brother. Richard had bequeathed financial bankruptcy and a ruinously expensive war in France. John also bore the brunt of baronial reaction to the centralization of government, a policy initiated by his predecessors, t
1 CONC hough continued with enthusiasm by him. 
1 CONT Early in his reign John lost most of the English possessions in France; by 1206, Philip II of France had conquered Anjou, Normandy, and Brittany. In that year John also became embroiled in a quarrel with the church by refusing to 
1 CONC accept the election of Stephen Langton as archbishop of Canterbury. The pope placed England under interdict (in effect, closing the churches) until John abandoned the fight in 1213 and accepted papal vassalage. 
1 CONT The king took this step to strengthen his hand against the barons, with whom trouble had been building since 1208. The failure of John's expedition to Poitou in 1214, however, coupled with the defeat of his ally, Holy Roman Empero
1 CONC r Otto IV, in the Battle of Bouvines, gave the English barons their excuse for rebellion. In June 1215 the barons forced the king to accede to their demands for the restoration of feudal rights in the famous document called Magna 
1 CONC Carta. The civil war was resumed soon after, however, and continued at the time of John's death on Oct. 18-19, 1216. John was succeeded by his young son, Henry III. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Author:  James W. Alexander
0 @N6000000001783522220@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT
1 CONT John was born at Beaumont Palace, Oxford, John was the fifth son and last of eight children born to Henry II of England and Eleanor of Aquitaine. Some authors, noting Henry's stay at Woodstock, near Oxford, with Eleanor in March 1
1 CONC 166, assert that John was born in that year, and not 1167.
1 CONT
1 CONT John was a younger maternal half-brother of Marie de Champagne and Alix of France, his mother's children by her first marriage to Louis VII of France, which was later annulled. He was a younger brother of William, Count of Poitier
1 CONC s; Henry the Young King; Matilda, Duchess of Saxony; Richard I of England; Geoffrey II, Duke of Brittany; Leonora, Queen of Castile; and Joan, Queen of Sicily.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry II had at first intended that John would receive an appropriate education to enter into the Church, which would have meant Henry did not have to apportion him land or other inheritance. In 1171, however, Henry began negotiat
1 CONC ions to betroth John to the daughter of Count Humbert III of Savoy (who had no son yet and so wanted a son-in-law.) After that, talk of making John a cleric ceased. John's parents had both received a good education - Henry spoke s
1 CONC ome half dozen languages, and Eleanor had attended lectures at what would soon become the University of Paris - in addition to what they had learned of law and government, religion, and literature. John himself had received one of
1 CONC  the best educations of any king of England. Some of the books the records show he read included: De Sacramentis Christianae Fidei by Hugh of St. Victor, Sentences by Peter Lombard, The Treatise of Origen, and a history of England
1 CONC -potentially Wace's Roman de Brut, based on Geoffrey of Monmouth's Historia Regum Britanniae.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriages and Children
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1189, John was married to Isabel of Gloucester, daughter and heiress of William Fitz Robert, 2nd Earl of Gloucester (she is given several alternative names by history, including Avisa, Hawise, Joan, and Eleanor). They had no ch
1 CONC ildren, and since her paternal grandfather was the illegitimate son of Henry I of England, John had their marriage annulled on the grounds of consanguinity, some time before or shortly after his accession to the throne, which took
1 CONC  place on 6 April 1199, and she was never acknowledged as queen. (She then married Geoffrey FitzGeoffrey de Mandeville, 2nd Earl of Essex as her second husband and Hubert de Burgh as her third).
1 CONT
1 CONT John remarried, on 24 August 1200, Isabella of Angoulême, who was twenty years his junior. She was the daughter of Aymer Taillefer, Count of Angouleme. John had kidnapped her from her fiancé, Hugh X of Lusignan. Isabella bore five
1 CONC  children:
1 CONT King Henry III of England (1207-1272).
1 CONT Richard, 1st Earl of Cornwall (1209-1272).
1 CONT Joan (1210-1238), Queen Consort of Alexander II of Scotland.
1 CONT Isabella (1214-1241), Consort of Frederick II, Holy Roman Emperor.
1 CONT Eleanor (1215-1275), who married William Marshal, 2nd Earl of Pembroke, and later married Simon de Montfort, 6th Earl of Leicester.
1 CONT
1 CONT Natural Children
1 CONT
1 CONT John is given a great taste for lechery by the chroniclers of his age, and even allowing some embellishment, he did have many illegitimate children. Matthew Paris accuses him of being envious of many of his barons and kinsfolk, an
1 CONC d seducing their more attractive daughters and sisters. Roger of Wendover describes an incident that occurred when John became enamoured of Margaret, the wife of Eustace de Vesci and an illegitimate daughter of King William I of S
1 CONC cotland. Eustace substituted a prostitute in her place when the king came to Margaret's bed in the dark of night; the next morning, when John boasted to Vesci of how good his wife was in bed, Vesci confessed and fled.
1 CONT
1 CONT John had the following illegitimate children (unless otherwise stated by unknown mistresses):
1 CONT Joan, Lady of Wales, the wife of Prince Llywelyn Fawr of Wales, (by a woman named Clemence)
1 CONT Richard Fitz Roy, (by his cousin, Adela, daughter of his uncle Hamelin de Warenne)
1 CONT Oliver FitzRoy, (by a mistress named Hawise) who accompanied the papal legate Pelayo to Damietta in 1218, and never returned.
1 CONT Geoffrey FitzRoy, who went on expedition to Poitou in 1205 and died there.
1 CONT John FitzRoy, a clerk in 1201.
1 CONT Henry FitzRoy, who died in 1245.
1 CONT Osbert Gifford, who was given lands in Oxfordshire, Norfolk, Suffolk, and Sussex, and is last seen alive in 1216.
1 CONT Eudes FitzRoy, who accompanied his half-brother Richard, Earl of Cornwall on Crusade and died in the Holy Land in 1241.
1 CONT Bartholomew FitzRoy, a member of the order of Friars Preachers.
1 CONT Maud FitzRoy, Abbess of Barking, who died in 1252.
1 CONT Isabel FitzRoy, wife of Richard Fitz Ives.
1 CONT Philip FitzRoy, found living in 1263.
1 CONT
1 CONT (The surname of FitzRoy is Norman-French for son of the king.)
1 CONT
1 CONT Civil Strife and Magna Carta
1 CONT
1 CONT Death
1 CONT
1 CONT Retreating from the French invasion, John took a safe route around the marshy area of the Wash to avoid the rebel held area of East Anglia. His slow baggage train (including the Crown Jewels), however, took a direct route across i
1 CONC t and was lost to the unexpected incoming tide. This loss dealt John a terrible blow, which affected his health and state of mind. Succumbing to dysentery and moving from place to place, he stayed one night at Sleaford Castle befo
1 CONC re dying on 18 October (or possibly 19 October) 1216, at Newark Castle (then in Lincolnshire, now on Nottinghamshire's border with that county). Numerous, possibly fictitious, accounts circulated soon after his death that he had b
1 CONC een killed by poisoned ale, poisoned plums or a "surfeit of peaches".
1 CONT
1 CONT He was buried in Worcester Cathedral in the city of Worcester.
1 CONT
1 CONT His nine-year-old son succeeded him and became King Henry III of England (1216-72), and although Louis continued to claim the English throne, the barons switched their allegiance to the new king, forcing Louis to give up his claim
1 CONC  and sign the Treaty of Lambeth in 1217.
