0 @N6000000003658760402@ NOTE From Plantagenet Ancestry:
1 CONT
1 CONT JOAN OF ENGLAND, born at Gloucester 22 July 1210.  In 1214 she was contracted to marry Geoffrey de Lusignan, son of Hugh de Lusignan, Count of la Marche, which marriage did not take place.  She married at York, Yorkshire 19 June 1
1 CONC 221 (as his 1st wife) ALEXANDER II, King of Scotland, Knt., son and heir of William the Lion, King of Scotland, by Ermengarde .... He was born at Haddington, East Lothian 24 August 1198.  They had no issue.  He was crowned at Scon
1 CONC e 6 Dec. 1214.  Joan died at York, Yorkshire 4 March 1237/8, buried at Tarrant Keynstan, Dorset.  Alexander II married (2nd) ... Mary de Coucy .... They had one son, Alexander (III) [King of Scotland].  Alexander II, King of Scotl
1 CONC and, died at Kerrera in the bay of Oban 8 July 1249, and was buried at Melrose Abbey.
0 @N6000000001783522119@ NOTE AKA: the Lady of Wales.
1 CONT
1 CONT Married to the Llewellyn the Great.  Natural daughter of king John of England.  Mother was
0 @N6000000002907048613@ NOTE illegitimately
0 @N6000000004303404083@ NOTE WIKIPEDIA: 
1 CONT
1 CONT Joan, Lady of Wales, (c.1191 - February 2, 1237) was the wife of Llywelyn the Great, Prince of Gwynedd and effective ruler of most of Wales.
1 CONT
1 CONT Early life
1 CONT Joan was an illegitimate daughter of King John of England and a woman named Clemence. She should not be confused with her legitimate half-sister Joan, Queen Consort of Scotland.
1 CONT
1 CONT Little is known about her early life; she was possibly born before her father, King John of England, married his first wife in 1189. Her mother's name is known only from Joan's obituary in the Tewkesbury Annals, where she is myste
1 CONC riously called "Regina Clementina" (Queen Clemence). Joan seems to have spent her childhood in France, as King John had her brought to the Kingdom of England from Normandy for her wedding in December 1203.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriage
1 CONT Joan married Llywelyn the Great between December 1203 and October 1204. She and Llywelyn had at least three children together:
1 CONT
1 CONT 1.	Gwladus Ddu (1206-1251), married (1) Reginald de Braose and (2) Ralph de Mortimer
1 CONT 2.	Elen ferch Llywelyn (1207-1253), married (1) John the Scot, Earl of Chester and (2) Robert de Quincy
1 CONT 3.	Dafydd ap Llywelyn (1208-1246) married Isabella de Broase, died at Garth Celyn, Aber Garth Celyn, (Aber).
1 CONT
1 CONT Some of Llywelyn's other recorded children may also have been Joan's:
1 CONT 1.	Susanna, who was sent to England as a hostage in 1228.
1 CONT In April 1226 Joan obtained a papal decree from Pope Honorius III, declaring her legitimate on the basis that her parents had not been married to others at the time of her birth, but without giving her a claim to the English thron
1 CONC e.
1 CONT
1 CONT At Easter 1230, William de Braose, Lord of Abergavenny, who was Llywelyn's nominal prisoner at the time, was discovered together with Joan in Llywelyn's bedchamber. William de Braose was hanged in the marshland at the foot of Gart
1 CONC h Celyn, the place known since as Gwern y Grog. Joan herself was placed out of public view, under virtual house arrest, for twelve months after the incident.
1 CONT
1 CONT Joan was never called Princess of Wales, but "Lady of Wales". She died at the royal home, Garth Celyn, Aber Garth Celyn, on the north coast of Gwynedd in 1237. Llywelyn's great grief at her death is recorded; he founded a Francisc
1 CONC an Friary on the seashore at Llanfaes, opposite the royal home, in her honour. The Friary was consecrated in 1240, shortly before Llywelyn died. It was closed down in 1537 by Henry_VIII_of_England in the Dissolution of the Monaste
1 CONC ries.
1 CONT Joan's stone coffin can be seen in Beaumaris parish church, Anglesey.
1 CONT
1 CONT Note
1 CONT In Welsh, Joan's name is written as "Siwan" because the language does not have the phonetic sound of the English 'j.'
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT •	Rotuli Litterarum Clausarum in Turri Londinensi I, p. 12.
1 CONT •	Luard, Henry. Annales Monastici 1, 1864
1 CONT •	Tewkesbury Annals
1 CONT
1 CONT -------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT WIKIPEDIA:  In the hope of avoiding trouble in England and Wales while he was away fighting to recover his French lands, in 1205, John formed an alliance by marrying off his illegitimate daughter, Joan, to the Welsh prince Llywely
1 CONC n the Great.
1 CONT
1 CONT -------------------
1 CONT
1 CONT Leo van de Pas posting 
1 CONT to soc.genealogy.medieval newsgroup, 23 Feb 1999:
1 CONT
1 CONT I found the following about Joan bastard of England, after her death she was buried at Llanfaes but, at the dissolution of the monastery, her coffin was removed. In the 19th century it was discovered that it was being used as a ho
1 CONC rse trough. Rescued from this use, it was placed in Baron Hill Park, near Beaumaris in Anglesey. The most recent location is given as the Church of St. Mary and St. Nicholas in Beaumaris.
