0 @N6000000003658751761@ NOTE From Plantagenet Ancestry:
1 CONT
1 CONT ELEANOR OF ENGLAND, born at Gloucester in 1215.  She was married (1st) (as his 2nd wife) by betrothal dated 23 April 1224 WILLIAM MARSHALL, 5th Earl of Pembroke, hereditary Master Marshal, Constable of Carmarthen and Cardigan Cast
1 CONC les, Justiciar of Ireland, son and heir of WIlliam Marshal, Knt., 4th Earl of Pembroke (or Striguil), by Isabel, daughter of Richard Fitz Gilbert (nicknamed Strongbow), 2nd Earl of Pembroke (or Striguil).  He was born about 1190, 
1 CONC in Normandy.  They had no issue.  WILLIAM MARSHALL ... died 6 April 1231, and was buried in the Temple Church, London.  She married (2nd) in the King's chapel at Westminster 7 Jan. 1237/8 SIMON DE MONTFORT, Earl of Leicester, High
1 CONC  Steward of England, Governor of Gascony, 3rd son of Simon de Montfort, Earl of Leicester, seigneur of Montfort, by Alice. ... He was bron about 1208.  They had five sons ... and two daughters. ... In 1229 his older brother, Amaur
1 CONC y de Montfort, surrendered to him all their father's inheritance in England.  In August 1231 King Henry III restored him ni his father's lands in England.  In 1236 he served as High Steward at Queen Eleanor's coronation.  In 1240 
1 CONC he embarked on a Crusade to the Holy Land and returned to Europe in Spring 1242.  He took part in King Henry III's unsuccessful expedition to Poitou in 1242.  IN 1253 he and his wife, Eleanor, were granted Kenilworth castle, Warwi
1 CONC ckshire, and the manor of Odiham, Hampshire for life.  In 1254 he was sent to Scotland on a secret missino for the king.  In 1263 Parliament denounced the king as "false to his oath" and declared war on all violators of the "Provi
1 CONC sions."  In July 1263 Earl Simon and the barons entered London, where he made the king ratify his concessions.  In 1264 a decisive battle was fought at Lewes, where the King and his son Edward were captured.  Simon subsequently ca
1 CONC rried on the government under the King's name and seal, including the summoning of a Parliament in London in Jan. 1265.  SIMON DE MONTFORT, Earl of Leicester, was slain at the Battle of Evesham 4 August 1265, and was buried at Eve
1 CONC sham Abbey.  His tomb at Evesham at once became a shrine at which miracles were wrought.  His widow, Eleanor, went into exile about Nov. 1265.  She retired to the nunnery of Montargis in France, where she died testate 13 April 127
1 CONC 5.
0 @N6000000005076836936@ NOTE Eleanor of England was born in 1215 at Gloucester, Gloucestershire, England.2 She was the daughter of John I 'Lackland', King of England and Isabella d'Angoulême. She married William Marshal, 5th Earl of Pembroke, son of William M
1 CONC arshal, 4th Earl of Pembroke and Isabella de Clare, Countess Strigoil, on 23 April 1224.2 She married Simon de Montfort, 6th Earl of Leicester, son of Simon de Montfort, 5th Earl of Leicester and Alice de Montmorency, on 7 January
1 CONC  1238.2 She died on 13 April 1275 at Montargis Abbey, Montargis, Orléanais, France.2 She was buried at Montargis Abbey, Montargis, Orléanais, France.2 She was buried at New Temple Church, London, England.
1 CONT      From 23 April 1224, her married name became Marshall. From 7 January 1238, her married name became de Montfort. She was a nun after 1265 at Montargis Abbey, Montargis, Orléanais, France.2
1 CONT Children of Eleanor of England and Simon de Montfort, 6th Earl of Leicester
1 CONT Peter de Montfort d. 1265
1 CONT Amaury de Montfort, Canon of York d. a Feb 13011
1 CONT Henry de Montfort b. 1238, d. 4 Aug 1265
1 CONT Simon de Montfort b. 1240, d. 12711
1 CONT Guy de Montfort, Count di Nola+ b. c 1243, d. c 12881
1 CONT Lady Eleanor de Montfort+ b. 1252, d. 12821
1 CONT Richard de Montfort b. b 1265, d. a 12661
