0 @N6000000002188376795@ NOTE Juan Manuel, Duke of Peñafiel
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Don Juan Manuel (Escalona, 5 May 1282 - Peñafiel, 13 June 1348) is one of the most important Spanish medieval writers, nephew of Alfonso X el Sabio, son of Infante Don Manuel de Castilla (Lord of Escalona and Peñafiel) and Lady Be
1 CONC atrice of Savoy.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Biography
1 CONT     * 2 Literature
1 CONT           o 2.1 Works
1 CONT     * 3 Children
1 CONT     * 4 Bibliography
1 CONT     * 5 See also
1 CONT     * 6 External links
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Biography
1 CONT
1 CONT Don Juan Manuel (1282-1348) was born in the Castle of Escalona in the province of Toledo and was a politician and very active soldier. He was son of Juan Manuel of Villena (the son of Fernando III Santo) and his second wife Beatri
1 CONC ce of Savoy. With the death of his mother Beatriz in 1292, Juan Manuel became duke of Peñafiel. Don Juan Manuel was trained in arts such as equestrianism, hunting, and fencing, and in addition learned Latin, history, law, and theo
1 CONC logy. At the age of twelve, he fought to repel the attack of the Moors from Granada to Murcia. He married three times, choosing his wives for political and economic convenience, and worked to match his children with partners assoc
1 CONC iated with royalty. Don Juan Manuel became one of the richest and most powerful men of his time, coining his own currency as the kings did. During his life, he was criticized for choosing literature as his vocation, an activity th
1 CONC ought below a nobleman of such prestige.
1 CONT
1 CONT Don Juan Manuel had constant confrontations with his king. At the time, the throne of Castile was occupied by two monarchs, Fernando IV and Alfonso XI. Juan Manuel's loyalty was with Alfonso, to whom Juan Manuel gave hand of his d
1 CONC aughter Constanza. The wedding was postponed several times, until finally Alfonso XI jailed Constanza in the Castle of Toro for unclear reasons. This incident angered Juan Manuel, who decided to turn against Alfonso. He declared w
1 CONC ar on Alfonso, beginning a confrontation that lasted five years.
1 CONT
1 CONT Finally the Pope brought about reconciliation between Juan Manuel and Alfonso XI. This reconciliation was not complete until 1340, when Juan Manuel and Alfonso allied against the Muslims in the Battle of Río Salado, taking the cit
1 CONC y of Algeciras. After these events, Juan Manuel left political life and retired to Murcia, where he spent his last years focused on literature. Proud of his works, he decided to compile them all in a single volume. This compilatio
1 CONC n was destroyed in a fire, with no known copy preserved.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1348, Juan Manuel died at the age of sixty-six years old. Throughout his life, he wrote approximately thirteen books, of which only eight are preserved today. These works are predominantly didactic.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Literature
1 CONT
1 CONT Following the path of his uncle, Alfonso X of Castile, Juan Manuel wrote in Castilian, a peculiarity in times when Latin was the 'official' language for educated writing. Juan Manuel wrote in the vulgar to facilitate access to lit
1 CONC erature for a greater number of readers.
1 CONT
1 CONT However, his writings were almost exclusively written for people of the nobility. His works reflect his character, ambitions, and beliefs. This is seen particularly in his Treaty of Asunción in which he defends the dogma of the Im
1 CONC maculate Conception.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Works
1 CONT
1 CONT Juan Manuel's work is marked by a great preoccupation with the body, soul, and intelligence of an ideal medieval horseman. Generally it is classified as material for education of princes.
1 CONT
1 CONT The structure of stories in El Conde Lucanor reflects the ordinances and hierarchical structuring of the medieval world. In the first parts a young nobleman, Lucanor, proposes an abstract problem to Patronio; later, he gives an ap
1 CONC ologue which extracts the solution from Patronio's tale, applying it to himself. Juan Manuel concludes the story with a short verse, condensing the moral of the story into short, concrete statements.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Children
1 CONT
1 CONT By Constanza of Aragon:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Constanza Manuel (c 1323-1345), who married Pedro I of Portugal.
1 CONT     * Beatrice, died young
1 CONT
1 CONT By Blanca Núñez de Lara:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Fernando Manuel (d c 1350), Lord of Escalona, Penafiel and Villena, who married 1346 Joana of Ampurias, a daughter of Ramón Berenguer, Count of Ampurias, himself a younger son of James II of Aragón. The couple had a daughter
1 CONC , Blanca Manuel (c 1348-1361), heiress of Villena, Escalona and Penafiel until 1361.
1 CONT     * Juana Manuel (1339-1381), who married 1350 Enrique II of Castile (1333-79) and became Queen of Castile.
1 CONT
1 CONT Illegitimate by Inés de Castañeda:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Sancho Manuel (1320-1347)
1 CONT     * Enrique Manuel (1340-1390)
1 CONT
1 CONT Illegitimate by unknown woman [1]:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Guiomar Manuel de Villena (who married Juan Martínez de Leiva, Señor de Baños, Merino Mayor de Castilla)
1 CONT     * Juana Manuel de Villena (who married Juan Galíndez de Avendaño, Señor de Mújica)
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Bibliography
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Ayerbe-Chaux, Reinaldo. ' ' Count Lucanor: Traditional matter and originality creadorá'. Madrid: J. Porrúa Turanzas, 1975. * Biglieri, Aníbal A. ' ' Towards a poetic one of the didactic story: Eight studies on count Lucanor.
1 CONC  Chapel Hill: UNC Dept. of Romance Languages, 1989.
1 CONT     * Flory, David. ' ' Count Lucanor: Don Juan Manuel in its context históricó'. Madrid: Sheets, 1995. * Giménez Soler, Andrés. ' ' Don Juan Manuel. Biography and study críticó'. Zaragoza: F. Martinez, 1932.
1 CONT     * Hammer, Michael Floyd. "Framing the Reader: Exemplarity and Ethics in the Manuscripts of the ' Count Lucanor'." Ph.D. University of Californian AT Los Angeles, 2004.
1 CONT     * Lida de Malkiel, Maria Rosa. "Three notes on Don Juan Manuel." ' ' Romance Philology' ' 4,2-3 (1950): 155-94. * Wacks, David A. "Don Yllán and the Egyptian Sorcerer: Vernacular commonality and literary diversity in medieval 
1 CONC Castile." ' ' Sefarad' ' 65,2 (2005): 413-33.
1 CONT     * MacPherson, Ian. ed. Juan Manuel: A Selection. London: Tamesis Texts Limited, 1980.
0 @N6000000002322288154@ NOTE He succeeded his father as King of naples, succeeded in turn by his
1 CONT granddaughter Joanna I, Queen of Naples 1343-1382, who adopted Louis of
1 CONT Anjou. Robert was an Anevin prince and Guelf (papal party) leader whose early
1 CONT years were clouded by the Sicilian Vespers (1282-1288) in which his father,
1 CONT Charles II of Anjou, was taken prisoner by the Aragonese. Robert took
1 CONT Charles's place as hostage. Later, Robert led an expedition attempting to
1 CONT recover Sicily from the Aragonese prince who ruled as Frederick III.
0 @N6000000002187820703@ NOTE Robert of Naples
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT King Robert I of Naples a.k.a. Robert the Wise, third but eldest surviving son of King Charles II of Naples the Lame, was Duke of Calabria (1296–1309), King of Naples and titular King of Jerusalem, and Count of Provence and Forcal
1 CONC quier (1309–1343).
1 CONT
1 CONT He was made King of Naples and Sicily in 1309, after the death of his father Charles II, his reign being blessed by Pope Clement V. While Robert's nephew Charles Robert of Anjou could as rightfully have succeeded, as the son of hi
1 CONC s elder brother, Charles Martel, i.e based on primogeniture, he was preoccupied with the capture of the Hungarian crown (which he accomplished in 1310) and did not press his claim on the crown. Robert was the heir in proximity of 
1 CONC blood.
1 CONT
1 CONT King Robert I was nicknamed "the peace-maker of Italy" due to the years of significant changes he made to Naples. Tradesmen from Italy and abroad erected superb buildings, monuments and statues that drastically changed King Robert
1 CONC 's capital from a dirty seaport to a city of elegance and medieval splendor.
1 CONT
1 CONT Head of the Guelph army at Genoa, King Robert reigned until his death in 1343. He was succeeded, his son Charles having predeceased him, by his 16-year-old granddaughter Joan I of Naples, who was already betrothed to 15-year-old A
1 CONC ndrew of Hungary, son of Charles Robert. However, in his will, he recognized the rights of Andrew to Naples as son of Charles Robert and ordained that he be crowned king in his own right. This provision was to result in fatal stri
1 CONC fe between Joan and Andrew.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT King Robert's marriages and descendants
1 CONT First wife, Yolanta or Yolanda of Aragon, daughter of King Peter III of Aragon 
1 CONT Charles (1298–1328), Duke of Calabria (1309), Viceroy of Naples (1318) 
1 CONT Louis (1301–1310) 
1 CONT Second wife, Sancha, daughter of King James II of Majorca 
1 CONT Unknown mistress 
1 CONT Fiametta -> married Andrea Thopia, Lord of Matija 
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Charles II King of Naples
1 CONT 1309–1343 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Joan I 
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Matilda Prince of Achaea
